{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md && sed -n '241,520p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md && sed -n '1,260p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/go-testing-tools/SKILL.md && sed -n '261,520p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/go-testing-tools/SKILL.md && for f in /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_PLATFORM.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_STRUCTURE.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_ATTACHMENTS.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_TOOLS.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_SKILLS.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_SKILL_TRIGGERS.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_DIAGRAMS.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_TESTING.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_WORKFLOW.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_DOCS.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_STYLE.md; do sed -n '1,260p' \\\"\"'$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md && sed -n '241,520p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md && sed -n '1,260p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/go-testing-tools/SKILL.md && sed -n '261,520p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/go-testing-tools/SKILL.md && for f in /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_PLATFORM.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_STRUCTURE.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_ATTACHMENTS.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_TOOLS.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_SKILLS.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_SKILL_TRIGGERS.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_DIAGRAMS.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_TESTING.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_WORKFLOW.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_DOCS.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_STYLE.md; do sed -n '1,260p' \\\"\"'$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"---\nname: project-management\ndescription: >\n  File-based task boards and spawn-first multi-agent orchestration for AI agents.\n  Use when creating or maintaining boards, decomposing specs into Epic -> Story\n  -> Task/Bug work, tracking bugs, checking status/progress, or coordinating\n  execution through task-board. For board-driven execution, the active role of a\n  normal non-spawned session is ORCHESTRATOR (coordinator/supervisor): plan from\n  the critical path, create and refine board items, then delegate research,\n  implementation, testing, docs, and review to tracked background child runs\n  with explicit role, model, and reasoning selections; child-agent type follows\n  the current orchestrator runtime unless the human or task context explicitly\n  overrides it. Before launch,\n  inspect the project spawn policy: spawn.enabled can disable all child runs and\n  per-agent model_criterion=equal pins the executable model without model ranking;\n  ordered criteria use provider-local model ranks and require deliberate\n  one-line selection rationale plus content-bound acknowledgement after any\n  requested-to-resolved adjustment.\n  Spec-to-board decomposition is the Orchestrator's inline duty: never create a\n  meta-task or spawn solution-architect solely for board layout. Do not perform\n  task work inline unless the human explicitly asks for inline execution, except\n  for this mandatory board-layout duty.\n  Russian triggers: борд, доска, задачи,\n  эпик, стори, таски, скил, спавн, оркестратор, субагент, агенты, критический\n  путь, декомпозиция, статус, прогресс, гол, голы, цель.\ntriggers:\n  - creating task board from spec\n  - breaking down spec into tasks\n  - project planning\n  - task management\n  - creating bugs\n  - bug tracking\n  - epic, story, task, board, task-board\n  - борд, доска, задачи, эпик, стори, таски\n  - спека, требования, декомпозиция, разбей на задачи\n  - что дальше, какие задачи, статус, прогресс, покажи борд\n  - spawn, orchestrator, coordinator, sub-agent, multi-agent\n  - goal, delivery goal, goal-scope, goal-bound pool\n  - спавн, оркестратор, координатор, субагент, агенты, критический путь\n  - гол, голы, цель, взять пул под гол\n  - work planning, what's next, status, progress, summary\n---\n\n# Project Management Skill\n\nFile-backed project management via `task-board` plus tracked child-process orchestration. Board state lives in git or a board-server; executable work is delegated through role-based spawned agents.\n\n## Active Role: Orchestrator\n\nYour active role is **Orchestrator**. In the `task-board` role registry, `orchestrator` and `coordinator` are the same supervisor identity; `coordinator` is the older implementation name.\n\nUnless the prompt explicitly gives you a spawned specialist role, `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` is set, or the human explicitly asks for inline execution, do not act as a worker. Act as the parent Orchestrator.\n\nThe Orchestrator does board and orchestration work only:\n- create/refine epics, stories, tasks, bugs, AC, checklist DoD, dependencies, and resources\n- inspect `project_config()`, `summary()`, `plan(..., active=true)`, `agents()`, and `spawn` run state\n- choose the first unblocked critical-path work and spawn the right specialist roles\n- own each selected item through producer -> reviewer -> rework -> reviewer cycles until a reviewer accepts it and the board reaches `done`\n- summarize child outcomes for routing/status and report status without delegating routine review back to the human\n\n### Inline Spec Decomposition\n\nSpec decomposition into board elements is the Orchestrator's own inline duty. Read the spec and directly create or refine its epics, stories, tasks/bugs, acceptance criteria, checklist DoD, and dependencies with `task-board`. This board-layout work is an explicit exception to the normal specialist-delegation rule.\n\nDo not create a meta-task whose sole purpose is to lay out a spec on the board. Do not spawn `solution-architect` merely to translate a settled spec into stories and tasks. A `solution-architect` spawn is valid only when the work contains a genuine architecture decision; after that decision, the Orchestrator still owns the resulting board layout inline.\n\nDelegation examples:\n\n- Invalid: create a `decompose-auth-spec` meta-task and spawn `solution-architect` solely to turn the settled auth spec into stories and tasks.\n- Invalid: spawn `solution-architect` to copy already-settled requirements, acceptance criteria, and dependencies into board elements.\n- Valid: spawn `solution-architect` to choose between event-driven and request/response integration across modules, then use the decision while laying out the board inline.\n- Valid: spawn `solution-architect` to define an ambiguous cross-module contract and ownership boundary before refining the affected stories and tasks inline.\n\nThis mandate avoids orchestration work that produces no executable scope: the wordstats probe spent two full `sol/max` sessions and 25 minutes on meta-delegated decomposition before the first real task existed.\n\nThe Orchestrator does not do specialist task work inline. The board-layout duty above is the explicit exception. Decision rule: if the next step would produce or validate a project artifact — research, code/config changes, tests, documentation, synthesis/decision docs, or a review verdict — prepare a clear board item and run a tracked background specialist:\n\n```bash\n# With no provider system or legacy preference: Codex keeps Codex affinity\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\n\n# With no provider system or legacy preference: Claude keeps Claude affinity\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-def456 --role researcher --background --model claude-opus-4-8\n\n# Explicit human/task-context override: pass --agent and override affinity\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-ghi789 --role reviewer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-4-8\n```\n\nUse `task-board spawn wait RUN-...` only at real dependency barriers. Parallelize independent work in the same phase; sequence producer -> reviewer -> dependent consumer work.\n\n## Delivery Ownership: Close the Loop\n\nOnce execution starts, the Orchestrator remains responsible for delivery:\n\n1. Monitor the producer until it reaches `to-review` with outcome evidence.\n2. Spawn and monitor a reviewer child. `to-review` is an immediate routing trigger, never a stopping point or a request for human review.\n3. If changes are requested, preserve the verdict as rework context, route to `to-dev` or `analysis`, and spawn the appropriate producer.\n4. When rework returns to `to-review`, spawn a new reviewer. Repeat until the reviewer accepts and the board item is `done`.\n5. If any child exits before its role handoff or leaves the item parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing`, inspect the run and outcomes, then retry or reroute through a focused child. Do not hand routine recovery to the human.\n6. Continue dependent work and close eligible parents. Report completion only after accepted `done` is real on the board.\n\nNever ask the human to perform routine code, test, documentation, research, or acceptance review. Human review is valid only when a task explicitly defines a human approval boundary. Requested changes, failed tests, recoverable child exits, and retryable runtime failures are orchestration work: retry, reroute, or spawn focused rework and keep going.\n\n## Project-State Monitoring\n\nUse the observability preset family for the Orchestrator's broad-to-narrow project-state loop. These are field presets on the existing query operations, not new operations:\n\n| Level | Query | Top-level projection |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Project | `summary() { observability-project }` | `observability` |\n| Epic | `list(type=epic) { observability-epic }` or `get(EPIC-...) { observability-epic }` | `id`, `name`, `status`, `observability` |\n| Story | `get(STORY-...) { observability-story }` | `id`, `name`, `status`, `observability` |\n| Task/Bug | `get(TASK-...) { observability-task }` | `id`, `name`, `status`, `observability` |\n\nUse compact output for routine monitoring:\n\n```bash\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary() { observability-project }'\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=epic) { observability-epic }'\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(STORY-260218-abc123) { observability-story }'\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260218-def456) { observability-task }'\n```\n\n`observability-task` is also the leaf preset for Bugs. All four presets expose one stable nested shape:\n\n- `observability.scope` is `{type:\"project\", name:\"Project\"}` at project level and `{id, type, name}` for an element subtree.\n- `observability.summary` preserves the existing summary shape, scoped to the project or selected element plus its descendants: `byType` status counts, `active` element rows, and `blocked` rows with `blockedBy`, `derivedBlockedBy`, `isBlocked`, and `isAdvisoryBlocked`.\n- `observability.readiness.estimateKind` is the leaf or calculated estimate state: `notEstimated`, `estimated`, or `partiallyEstimated` as applicable.\n- `observability.readiness.coverage` always contains numeric `estimated`, `unestimated`, and `total`, string `ratio` such as `\"2/3\"`, and boolean `complete`.\n- `observability.readiness.rolledEstimate` is always the known sum shape `{kind:\"estimated\", scale:{kind:\"fibonacci\", number:N}}`, including when coverage is partial.\n- `observability.readiness.isBlocked` / `blockedElementIds` aggregate hard blocking in the scope. `isAdvisoryBlocked` / `advisoryBlockedElementIds` separately aggregate derived cross-container waiting. The ID arrays are sorted.\n\nPlain `summary()` keeps its legacy `{summary:{byType, active, blocked}}` response. Select `observability-project` when the readiness fields are needed.\n\n### Monitoring and routing loop\n\n1. **Project readiness:** start with `summary() { observability-project }`. Read `summary.active` for work that needs supervision or review routing, `summary.blocked` for blocker details, and `readiness` for aggregate estimate coverage and hard/advisory state. This is the fastest board-wide routing view.\n2. **Epic readiness:** list `observability-epic` rows to identify which delivery subtree owns a partial-coverage or blocker signal. Compare the top-level Epic `status` with its scoped `summary.active`; a quiet-looking container can still have active descendants, and a live container can still contain uncovered estimates.\n3. **Story readiness:** query the affected Story with `observability-story`. Story coverage is over its direct Task/Bug leaves; use the scoped active and blocked rows to decide which producer, reviewer, or blocker should move next.\n4. **Task/Bug readiness:** query the next leaf with `observability-task`. Coverage `1/1` plus no hard block means its estimate precondition is covered, but launch order still comes from dependencies and `plan(..., active=true)`. Coverage `0/1` or `estimateKind=notEstimated` means a new leaf needs an explicit estimate before entering `development`.\n\nWhen any project, Epic, or Story view reports `coverage.complete=false` or `estimateKind=partiallyEstimated`, do not call the rolled estimate a full-scope estimate. Narrow to incomplete Epics/Stories, then request the separate estimate projection:\n\n```bash\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(EPIC-260218-abc123) { estimate }'\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(STORY-260218-def456) { estimate }'\n```\n\nThe partial `estimate` projection adds the exact `notEstimatedCount` and sorted `unestimatedTaskIds` to its known `rolledEstimate`. Set or route estimates for those concrete Task/Bug IDs before using the roll-up for capacity or scope decisions. Missing estimates are missing coverage, not zero-point work. Legacy leaves without a persisted Estimate section remain start-compatible, but still appear as unestimated in container projections.\n\nRead blocker signals independently:\n\n- `isBlocked=true` means at least one scoped element is hard-blocked; inspect `blockedElementIds` and the matching `summary.blocked[*].blockedBy`, then advance those manual blockers before launching the dependent leaf.\n- `isAdvisoryBlocked=true` means at least one scoped element is waiting because of derived cross-container ordering; inspect `advisoryBlockedElementIds` and `derivedBlockedBy`, schedule the source first, but do not treat advisory state as status enforcement.\n- Both flags may be true in the same scope. Never collapse a mixed signal into one generic blocked label.\n\n`coverage.complete` describes estimate coverage only; it is not a delivery-ready or accepted verdict. `rolledEstimate` sums all known scoped leaf estimates regardless of lifecycle status, so it is not remaining work. Combine observability with `plan(..., active=true)` for waves/critical path, `agents()` for assignment state, and `spawn status/events/watch/wait` for child-run health.\n\n## Stop-The-Line: No Forced Fits\n\nDo not keep implementing when the task starts requiring a forced fit. A forced fit is any path where the agent has discovered that the requested behavior conflicts with a platform/API constraint, product decision, UX state model, ownership boundary, or architecture, and the remaining \"solution\" is mostly compensating hacks.\n\nThe Bluetooth permission/off-state failure pattern is the canonical warning sign: once the agent learned that iOS could not reveal powered-off Bluetooth state before permission without touching CoreBluetooth and potentially triggering the system prompt, it should have stopped, documented the constraint and options, and asked for a product decision. Adding more booleans, stubs, priority rules, and UI tests only made an invalid model look temporarily testable.\n\nPause or escalate only for either an evidence-backed external blocker that cannot be resolved autonomously or an evidence-backed unresolved human-only boundary—an impossible platform/API or product constraint, approval boundary, or unresolved product or architecture tradeoff—that cannot be derived from the task, AC, or existing decisions.\n\nWhen this happens:\n- Stop changing product code before adding another workaround layer.\n- Record the constraint, evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommended option, and exact decision or external input needed on the board as notes or an outcome resource.\n- Mark the task `blocked` and request only that exact decision or external input. Do not use `blocked` for recoverable failures or ordinary rework.\n- Resume only after the product/architecture decision is explicit, or after you find a genuinely clean design that does not depend on the broken assumption.\n\nTests and stubs are not proof that a forced-fit design is correct. They are useful only after the state model and platform assumptions are valid.\nRoutine review, rework, test failures, recoverable child-process failures, and implementation choices resolvable from existing requirements are not reasons to hand control back to the human.\n\n---\n\n# Part 1: Task Management\n\n---\n\n## When to Use\n\n- New project with SPEC/requirements → break down into epics/stories/tasks\n- Need progress tracking in files\n- Working with AI agent on a project\n- Tracking bugs\n\n---\n\n## CLI Tool: `task-board`\n\nAll board operations go through the `task-board` CLI. No manual file editing.\n\n**Source repo:** your local `skill-project-management` checkout\n**Installed skill artifact:** `~/.agents/skills/project-management` (degitized copy for agent runtimes)\n**In PATH:** installed copy in `~/.local/bin/task-board`\n\nInstall/update the local binary before board setup:\n\n```bash\ncd /path/to/skill-project-management\n./scripts/setup.sh\n```\n\nFor a safe reinstall that refreshes binaries, the installed skill copy, roles, and install metadata without touching the current project's `task-board.config.json`, use:\n\n```bash\n./scripts/setup.sh --install-only\n```\n\n`--install-only` is also the supported verification gate for rollout drift. It checks the installed `task-board` binary with `project_config()` and local `models()` queries, requires the tracked background, explicit-selection, and child launch-composition contract fields plus current Codex registry to be present, verifies the installed `SKILL.md` matches this source artifact, and fails loud if PATH resolves a stale binary.\n\n`task-board self-update` relies on `~/.config/task-board/install.json`, which is written by `scripts/setup.sh`. Do not treat `~/.agents/skills/project-management` as the update source; it is the installed artifact copy and has git metadata scrubbed.\n\n`scripts/setup.sh` is the single supported install/update entrypoint for `task-board`, `task-board-tui`, and the installed skill artifact. `make install-*` targets are binary build helpers and do not replace setup.\n\nWhen you bootstrap board-driven work inside a real code repository, prefer a single source-checkout setup command that also bootstraps the repo-local agent runtime through `relux-agents-infra`:\n\n```bash\ncd /path/to/skill-project-management\n./scripts/setup.sh --project-dir /path/to/project --mode local --board-dir .task-board --bootstrap-local-agents\n```\n\n`--bootstrap-local-agents` runs `agents-infra setup local /path/to/project` after writing the project config. Install the global `agents-infra` launcher once from the `relux-agents-infra` source repo via `./setup.sh`, then use this shortcut for project repos.\n\nThat local setup makes project `.agents/` the installed runtime copy and refreshes `.claude/` + `.codex/` as thin shims into it. Do this right after board/config setup so spawned agents see the repo-local instructions, skills, and templates instead of drifting onto only global state.\n\n### Quick Reference\n\nAll reads use `q` (query DSL), all writes use `m` (mutate DSL). Compact format (`--format compact`) saves tokens.\n\n```bash\n# Queries (reads)\ntask-board q 'schema()'                                                          # discover fields, presets, operations, mutations\ntask-board q 'project_config()'                                                  # effective runtime + spawn.enabled and per-agent model criteria; run before spawning/audits\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary()'                                        # board overview (counts + active/blocked)\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260218-abc123) { full }'                 # full element details\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { overview }'  # filtered list\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, blocked=true) { id name status }'  # blocked tasks\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=story, parent=EPIC-260218-abc123) { overview }'  # stories in epic\ntask-board q --format compact 'agents()'                                         # agent dashboard\ntask-board q --format compact 'models(agent=codex)'                             # local spawn model/effort registry (also in remote board mode)\ntask-board q --format compact 'models(agent=qwen)'                              # Qwen model/effort/context/Token Plan registry\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan(EPIC-260218-abc123, mode=children)'          # execution waves (super-compact planning shape)\ntask-board q 'plan(scope=[EPIC-260218-abc123, STORY-260218-def456], mode=children)'  # plan over a set of subtrees (union)\ntask-board q --format compact 'count(type=task, status=done)'                    # count elements\n\n# Mutations (writes)\ntask-board m 'create(type=epic, name=my-feature, description=\"...\")'                        # create epic\ntask-board m 'create(type=story, parent=EPIC-260218-abc123, name=audio-capture)'            # create story\ntask-board m 'create(type=task, parent=STORY-260218-abc123, name=impl, description=\"...\")'  # create task\ntask-board m 'set_details(TASK-260218-abc123, title=\"new title\", description=\"...\")'        # update README fields\ntask-board m 'rename(TASK-260218-abc123, name=better-task-name)'                            # update overview name/slug without changing ID\ntask-board m 'set_estimate(TASK-260218-abc123, estimate=estimated, scale=fibonacci, number=5)'  # estimate Task/Bug work\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260218-abc123, status=development)'                           # change status\ntask-board m 'set_status(STORY-260218-abc123, status=done, commit_ack=scope_committed)'      # acknowledge an enforced version-control done transition\ntask-board m 'assign(TASK-260218-abc123, agent=claude-opus)'                                # assign agent\ntask-board m 'unassign(TASK-260218-abc123)'                                                 # remove assignment\ntask-board m 'link(TASK-260218-def456, blocked_by=TASK-260218-abc123)'                      # add dependency\ntask-board m 'unlink(TASK-260218-def456, blocked_by=TASK-260218-abc123)'                    # remove dependency\ntask-board m 'check_item(TASK-260218-abc123, item=1)'                                       # check checklist item\ntask-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-260218-abc123, text=\"Write tests\")'                   # add checklist item\ntask-board m 'set_notes(TASK-260218-abc123, text=\"Progress update\")'                        # append notes\ntask-board m 'set_notes(TASK-260218-abc123, text=\"Full replacement\", set=true)'             # replace notes\ntask-board m 'set_parent(TASK-260218-abc123, parent=STORY-260218-def456)'                   # move to new parent\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md, content=\"...\", type=precondition, description=\"Architecture design\")'  # create resource\ntask-board m 'update_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md, content=\"revised\", type=precondition, description=\"Architecture design v2\")'  # replace resource\ntask-board m --dry-run 'delete(TASK-260218-abc123)'                                         # preview destructive op\ntask-board m --confirm 'delete(TASK-260218-abc123)'                                         # execute destructive op\n\n# Batching (semicolons)\ntask-board m 'assign(TASK-260218-abc123, agent=claude-opus); set_status(TASK-260218-abc123, status=development)'\n\n# Spawn tracked background runs (--role, --background, and --model are REQUIRED; Codex/Qwen also require --reasoning-effort)\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role researcher --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max --instruction impl-guide.md\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-4-8 --budget 5.0\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent qwen --model qwen3.7-plus --reasoning-effort xhigh\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-luna --reasoning-effort max\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.3-codex-spark --reasoning-effort xhigh\ntask-board spawn status RUN-260320-abc123                                            # inspect tracked run state\ntask-board spawn wait RUN-260320-abc123                                              # block on tracked run state after RUN exists\ntask-board spawn events RUN-260320-abc123                                            # replay durable run events (supports --cursor/--follow)\ntask-board spawn watch RUN-260320-abc123                                             # follow durable run-event notifications\n\n# Auth (always local, never sent to server)\ntask-board login sampleuser tb_4lc6...                                        # store token in Keychain and select active user outside repo config\ntask-board login admin tb_UJTW... --remote https://board.example.com          # explicit remote bootstrap\ntask-board logout                                                              # remove selected user's token\ntask-board whoami                                                              # show remote URL + active user + token status\ntask-board switch-user admin                                                   # switch active user without touching versioned config\ntask-board q 'whoami()'                                                        # whoami via query DSL\ntask-board q 'project_config()'                                                # mode, board source, and effective spawn policy\ntask-board m 'login(user=sampleuser, token=tb_xxx)'                           # login via mutation DSL\ntask-board m 'switch_user(user=admin)'                                         # switch via mutation DSL\n\n# CLI utilities\ntask-board grep \"AudioRecorder\"                            # full-text search across board files (local only)\ntask-board validate                                        # check board structure (local only)\ntask-board repair-links                                    # list unsupported stored container links (local, read-only)\ntask-board repair-links STORY-260218-abc123 --confirm      # remove selected suspicious links\ntask-board tui                                             # interactive terminal dashboard\n```\n\n---\n\n## Task Board Structure\n\n### Hierarchy\n\n```\nEpic → Story → Task/Bug\n```\n\n- **Epic** = large feature / module\n- **Story** = specific functionality within epic\n- **Task** = atomic unit of work\n- **Bug** = defect tracked within a story\n\n### Naming Convention\n\n**Distributed IDs** — each element gets a unique ID based on date + random hash.\n\n```\nEPIC-260203-a1b2c3_recording/\n  STORY-260203-d4e5f6_audio-capture/\n    TASK-260203-g7h8i9_audiorecorder-interface/\n    TASK-260203-j0k1l2_implementation/\n    BUG-260203-m3n4o5_some-bug/\n```\n\nFormat: `{TYPE}-{YYMMDD}-{hash}_{kebab-case-name}`\n- `EPIC-YYMMDD-xxxxxx_name`\n- `STORY-YYMMDD-xxxxxx_name`\n- `TASK-YYMMDD-xxxxxx_name`\n- `BUG-YYMMDD-xxxxxx_name`\n\nWhere:\n- `YYMMDD` — creation date (year, month, day)\n- `xxxxxx` — 6-character base36 hash (collision-resistant)\n\nIDs are globally unique and work in parallel environments (no counter conflicts).\n\n### Directory Layout\n\n```\n.task-board/\n├── .resources/                   # Flat resource storage (managed by CLI)\n│   ├── TASK-260203-g7h8i9/       # Resources keyed by element ID\n│   │   ├── design.md             # Precondition: input for the task\n│   │   └── results.md            # Outcome: work product\n│   └── STORY-260203-d4e5f6/\n│       └── research.md\n├── EPIC-260203-a1b2c3_recording/\n│   ├── README.md                 # Epic description, scope, AC\n│   ├── progress.md               # Status, blockedBy, checklist, notes, resource refs\n│   ├── STORY-260203-d4e5f6_audio-capture/\n│   │   ├── README.md\n│   │   ├── progress.md\n│   │   ├── TASK-260203-g7h8i9_interface/\n│   │   │   ├── README.md\n│   │   │   └── progress.md\n│   │   ├── TASK-260203-j0k1l2_implementation/\n│   │   │   ├── README.md\n│   │   │   └── progress.md\n│   │   └── BUG-260203-m3n4o5_crash-on-start/\n│   │       ├── README.md\n│   │       └── progress.md\n│   └── STORY-260203-p6q7r8_amplitude/\n│       └── ...\n└── EPIC-260203-s9t0u1_storage/\n    └── ...\n```\n\nNo `system.md` needed — IDs are self-contained and don't require global counters.\n\n---\n\n## Statuses\n\n9 statuses: `backlog` → `analysis` → `to-dev` → `development` → `to-review` → `reviewing` → `done`. Plus `closed` and `blocked`.\n\nBefore a newly created Task/Bug enters `development`, set its estimate with\n`set_estimate(ID, estimate=estimated, scale=fibonacci, number=N)`. The start\ntransition returns a typed estimate-required error while the explicit value is\n`notEstimated`. Legacy Task/Bug files that have no Estimate section are\ngrandfathered and remain startable.\n\n`done` is accepted leaf work or computed container completion; `closed` is an\nexplicit administrative disposition. A story or epic may remain `closed` only\nwhile every direct and transitive descendant is `done` or `closed`.\n\nFor full status flow, auto-promotion/reopen, blocked vs is_blocked — see [`references/statuses.md`](references/statuses.md).\n\n---\n\n## File Formats\n\nEach element directory contains two files:\n- **README.md** — static description (what the element is, scope, acceptance criteria). Does not change during execution.\n- **progress.md** — dynamic lifecycle journal (status, Task/Bug estimate, assignment, timestamps, dependencies, checklist, notes). Updated throughout execution.\n\nBoth files use source-aware, injection-safe Markdown persistence. Unchanged\nbytes and custom sections are retained during rewrites; free-text payload fields\nround-trip byte-exact. Mutation boundaries normalize identifier-like fields\n(`AssignedTo`, `BlockedBy`, `Blocks`) by trimming boundary whitespace and reject\nempty or reserved `(none)` values with a typed error. Checklist text is rejected\nonly when empty-after-trim and is otherwise persisted verbatim.\n\nTask/bug dependencies are bidirectional ground truth. Story/epic ordering is derived from the live task DAG at read time; task links never materialize container side-effect links. Explicit stored container links remain manual state, while projections expose task-derived blockers separately.\n\nFor detailed format specifications, field descriptions, and examples, see [`references/file-formats.md`](references/file-formats.md).\n\n---\n\n## Resources & Bugs\n\nResources are linked to board elements via DSL mutations. Two types:\n- **Precondition** — inputs for the task (instructions, specs, design docs). Used by `task-board spawn` to compose agent prompts.\n- **Outcome** — work products (research results, implementation notes). Persisted for other agents/tasks to consume.\n\n```bash\n# Create resource\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md, content=\"...\", type=precondition, description=\"Architecture design\")'\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_results.md, content=\"...\", type=outcome, description=\"Research findings\")'\n\n# Replace existing resource\ntask-board m 'update_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_results.md, content=\"revised findings\", type=outcome, description=\"Research findings v2\")'\n\n# Delete resource\ntask-board m --confirm 'delete_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md)'\n```\n\nResources are stored flat in `.task-board/.resources/<elementID>/` and referenced from `progress.md`. Bugs live inside stories alongside tasks.\n\nFor resource workflow and bug tracking — see [`references/resources-and-bugs.md`](references/resources-and-bugs.md).\n\n---\n\n## Workflow\n\n### Full Development Cycle (MANDATORY)\n\nWhen the user asks to build/implement something, the agent MUST follow this end-to-end flow:\n\n#### Spec\n- Specs live in the `.spec/` directory (one or more `.md` files), not in a single `SPEC.md`\n- Create `.spec/` if it doesn't exist; add focused spec files per topic/feature (e.g. `.spec/auth.md`, `.spec/api.md`)\n- Clarify requirements with the user if anything is ambiguous\n- `.spec/` is the source of truth — all work traces back to it\n\n#### Plan on the Board\n- Create epics and stories: `task-board m 'create(type=epic, name=..., description=\"...\")'`\n- Refine each element: query with `task-board q 'get(ID) { full }'`, clarify gaps, update via `task-board m 'set_details(ID, description=\"...\")'`\n- Perform spec-to-board decomposition inline as the Orchestrator; do not create a meta-task or spawn `solution-architect` merely to lay out a settled spec\n- No element should have placeholder text — description and AC must be complete and unambiguous\n\n#### Story Detailing, Phase Planning, Execution (MANDATORY)\n\nAfter the Orchestrator completes story detailing inline, use [`references/planning-workflow.md`](references/planning-workflow.md) for phase planning, user approval, pre-launch checks, specialist execution, supervision, and visualization. Its legacy decomposition-spawn examples do not override this inline mandate and must not be used for settled-spec board layout.\n\nKey points:\n- **Story detailing before planning** — the Orchestrator creates and refines the required stories and tasks inline before phase planning\n- **No meta-delegated board layout** — do not create a meta-task or spawn `solution-architect` solely to translate a settled spec into board elements; reserve that role for genuine architecture decisions such as system-design tradeoffs or unresolved cross-module contracts\n- **Use `task-board q 'plan(...)'` as the only planning source** — default JSON returns nested phases and `criticalPath`; compact output returns flat `waves` and a single-line `criticalPath`. Unresolvable dependencies fail with a typed validation error instead of producing misleading phases. Agents never need a rendered graph to plan. Use `task-board plan --save` only to persist a human-readable snapshot; never build phases manually or write plan files by hand\n- **Dump plan to `.planning/`** — after planning, save to `.planning/{YYMMDD}_{HHmmss}_{topic}.md` for session recovery. Use `task-board plan --save`\n- **User approval MANDATORY** — never start execution without user confirmation\n- **Pre-launch checklist** — model, status, assignment, concrete IDs, first command\n- **Coordinator is SUPERVISOR** — never writes code, only delegates to sub-agents\n\n#### Research / Analysis Tasks\n\nResearch workflow, resource linking, document structure, status flow — see [`references/research-workflow.md`](references/research-workflow.md).\n\nKey points:\n- Research results are linked to board elements via `task-board m 'add_resource(..., type=outcome)'` on first write and `update_resource(...)` on revisions\n- Broadly useful research also persists in **`.research/`** directory (project-level)\n- Status flow: `backlog → analysis → to-review → reviewing → done` (skip `development`; after `to-review`, spawn an explicit reviewer such as `task-board spawn TASK-... --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`)\n- Always link research output as an outcome resource on the task, and include the task ID in the resource name\n\n### Iterative Refinement (for individual elements)\n\nWhen creating ANY element (epic/story/task/bug), the agent MUST:\n\n1. **Create** element via `task-board m 'create(type=..., name=..., description=\"...\")'`\n2. **Read** the element: `task-board q 'get(ID) { full }'`\n3. **Clarify** — if description is vague or has gaps, ask the user\n4. **Update** via `task-board m 'set_details(ID, description=\"...\", ac=\"...\")'`\n5. **Repeat** steps 2-4 until description is exhaustive\n6. Element is **ready** only when description and AC are complete and unambiguous\n\n### Project Start\n\n- Create elements: `task-board m 'create(type=epic, name=auth-system, description=\"...\")'`\n- Create stories: `task-board m 'create(type=story, parent=EPIC-260218-abc, name=login)'`\n- Detail tasks: `task-board m 'create(type=task, parent=STORY-260218-abc, name=impl)'`\n- Set dependencies: `task-board m 'link(STORY-260218-def, blocked_by=STORY-260218-abc)'`\n- **Generate phases**: `task-board q 'plan(EPIC-260218-abc, mode=children)'` — only after all stories have tasks and dependencies are set\n\n### Spawning Agents for Tasks\n\nThe **coordinator never writes code** — it delegates through tracked spawn commands with explicit role/model selection and runtime-affine agent selection.\n\nTreat [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) as the canonical operator-facing spawn contract. The guidance below is the workflow summary agents should follow.\n\n**`--role`, `--background`, and `--model` are REQUIRED.** Codex and Qwen spawns also require `--reasoning-effort`; Claude spawns do not accept it. Choose the role based on the task:\n\n`--agent` is optional by design. Canonical `spawn.preferred_agentic_system` is an enforced tagged union: `exclusive` pins one provider; `mixed` permits a normalized provider set and retains runtime affinity when it belongs to that set. An explicit `--agent` wins only within the configured set; an out-of-set explicit provider or affinity is refused before task lookup or spawn side effects and names the allowed providers. When the canonical system is absent, legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` retains its ceiling/executable-gated preference and affinity fallback; when both are absent, a Codex orchestrator session spawns a `codex` child and a Claude orchestrator session spawns a `claude` child. The implementation detects Codex from `CODEX_SESSION`, `CODEX_THREAD_ID`, or `CODEX_CI` and otherwise uses Claude.\n\nOptional `spawn.schedule.windows[]` overlays that base provider system during disjoint UTC windows. Each window is start-inclusive/end-exclusive, uses `HH:MM`, may cross midnight, and carries its own `preferred_agentic_system`; equal or overlapping bounds are invalid. The active window replaces the base system before provider and registered-model validation, while gaps use the base system or unrestricted runtime affinity when no base exists. Inspect `project_config().spawn.schedule` for the configured windows, `active_window`, and `base_preferred_agentic_system` before selecting a provider.\n\n### Goal-Capable Primary Sessions\n\nThe fixed primary-session UX is `agents-infra codex` for Codex and\n`agents-infra claude` for Claude. Both are fully capable of starting\ngoal-bound tracked owners through `task-board spawn --goal-scope`.\n---\nname: go-testing-tools\ndescription: >\n  Go testing toolkit with tuitestkit library and a closed-loop development\n  cycle for Go applications. Covers test helpers, reducer tests,\n  snapshot/golden testing, mock executor patterns, and view assertions.\ntriggers:\n  - go test\n  - bubbletea test\n  - tuitestkit\n  - go test helpers\n  - reducer test\n  - snapshot test go\n  - golden file go\n  - тесты го\n  - го тест хелперы\n  - тесты баблти\n  - тест утилиты го\n  - замкнутый цикл тестирования\n---\n\n# Go Testing Tools\n\nTesting toolkit for Go TUI applications built with bubbletea. Provides a Go library (`tuitestkit`), reference documentation, and templates that enable a closed-loop development cycle: write code, write tests, run tests, validate, fix, repeat -- all without human intervention.\n\nCore philosophy: **Elm architecture makes TUI testable.** The reducer is a pure function -- given the same state and action, it always returns the same result. No I/O, no randomness, no terminal. Test everything as function calls.\n\n---\n\n## Prerequisites\n\n- **Go 1.21+**\n- **bubbletea** (`github.com/charmbracelet/bubbletea`)\n- **lipgloss** (`github.com/charmbracelet/lipgloss`) -- used by most bubbletea apps for styling\n\nInstall the library in your project:\n\n```bash\ngo get github.com/relux-works/skill-go-testing-tools/tuitestkit\n```\n\n---\n\n## Quick Start\n\nMinimal test showing the core workflow: build a message, send it to a model, assert on the view.\n\n```go\npackage myscreen_test\n\nimport (\n    \"testing\"\n\n    \"github.com/relux-works/skill-go-testing-tools/tuitestkit\"\n)\n\nfunc TestBasicNavigation(t *testing.T) {\n    m := NewMyModel(testData)\n    m = tuitestkit.Send(m, tuitestkit.WindowSize(80, 24))\n\n    // Press down twice\n    m = tuitestkit.Send(m, tuitestkit.Key(\"down\"), tuitestkit.Key(\"down\"))\n\n    // Assert the view contains expected content\n    tuitestkit.ViewContains(t, m, \"third item\")\n    tuitestkit.ViewNotContains(t, m, \"ERROR\")\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Testing Pyramid\n\nFive levels, from fastest/most numerous to slowest/fewest:\n\n| Level | What | Speed | Investment |\n|-------|------|-------|------------|\n| 1. Pure Reducer | `func(State, Action) State` | Nanoseconds | ~60% |\n| 2. Component | `Update` + `View` | Microseconds | ~20% |\n| 3. Integration | Multi-component with mocks | Milliseconds | ~10% |\n| 4. Snapshot | Golden file comparison | Milliseconds | ~5% |\n| 5. Behavioral | Full user workflows | Milliseconds | ~5% |\n\nWhen time is limited: **Level 1 > Level 2 > Level 3**. Pure reducer tests catch the most bugs per line of test code.\n\nFull details: `references/testing-pyramid.md`\n\n---\n\n## Library API Reference\n\nAll functions are in the `tuitestkit` package.\n\n### Message Builders (`messages.go`)\n\nBuild `tea.Msg` values for driving model updates.\n\n```go\n// Key builds a tea.KeyMsg from a human-readable string.\n// Supports: \"enter\", \"tab\", \"esc\", \"space\", \"backspace\", \"up\", \"down\",\n// \"left\", \"right\", \"home\", \"end\", \"pgup\", \"pgdown\", \"delete\", \"insert\",\n// \"f1\"-\"f20\", \"ctrl+c\", \"ctrl+a\"...\"ctrl+z\", \"alt+h\", \"alt+enter\",\n// single runes: \"a\", \"/\", \"1\", etc.\nfunc Key(k string) tea.KeyMsg\n\n// Keys builds a slice of tea.Msg from multiple key strings.\nfunc Keys(keys ...string) []tea.Msg\n\n// WindowSize builds a tea.WindowSizeMsg with the given dimensions.\nfunc WindowSize(w, h int) tea.WindowSizeMsg\n\n// MouseClick builds a tea.MouseMsg for a left-button click at (x, y).\nfunc MouseClick(x, y int) tea.MouseMsg\n\n// MouseClickRight builds a tea.MouseMsg for a right-button click at (x, y).\nfunc MouseClickRight(x, y int) tea.MouseMsg\n\n// MouseScroll builds a tea.MouseMsg for a scroll event.\n// Direction constants: ScrollUp, ScrollDown, ScrollLeft, ScrollRight.\nfunc MouseScroll(dir ScrollDir) tea.MouseMsg\n\n// MouseRelease builds a tea.MouseMsg for a button release at (x, y).\nfunc MouseRelease(x, y int) tea.MouseMsg\n```\n\n### Model Harness (`harness.go`)\n\nSend messages to bubbletea models and handle commands.\n\n```go\n// Send sends messages to a model sequentially, returns the final model.\n// Preserves concrete type via generics -- no type assertion needed.\n// Panics if Update returns a different type (broken implementation).\n// Init() is NOT called.\nfunc Send[M tea.Model](model M, msgs ...tea.Msg) M\n\n// SendAndCollect sends messages, collects all non-nil Cmds returned by Update.\n// Returns final model + collected commands.\nfunc SendAndCollect[M tea.Model](model M, msgs ...tea.Msg) (M, []tea.Cmd)\n\n// ExecCmds executes tea.Cmd functions synchronously, collects resulting messages.\n// Recursively handles tea.BatchMsg. Nil cmds/messages are skipped.\nfunc ExecCmds(cmds ...tea.Cmd) []tea.Msg\n```\n\n**Common pattern -- command pipeline:**\n\n```go\nm, cmds := tuitestkit.SendAndCollect(m, tuitestkit.Key(\"r\"))\nmsgs := tuitestkit.ExecCmds(cmds...)\nm = tuitestkit.Send(m, msgs...)\n```\n\n### Reducer Harness (`reducer.go`)\n\nTable-driven testing for pure reducer functions.\n\n```go\n// ReducerTest defines a single test case for a pure reducer.\n// S = state type, A = action type.\ntype ReducerTest[S any, A any] struct {\n    Name    string\n    Initial S\n    Action  A\n    Assert  func(t *testing.T, got S)\n}\n\n// Step defines one step in a multi-action reducer sequence.\n// Assert is optional -- if nil, applied without per-step validation.\ntype Step[S any, A any] struct {\n    Name   string\n    Action A\n    Assert func(t *testing.T, got S)\n}\n\n// ReducerSequence defines a multi-step test scenario.\n// Actions applied sequentially from Initial. Final runs on end state.\ntype ReducerSequence[S, A any] struct {\n    Name    string\n    Initial S\n    Steps   []Step[S, A]\n    Final   func(t *testing.T, got S)\n}\n\n// RunReducerTests executes table-driven reducer tests as subtests.\nfunc RunReducerTests[S, A any](t *testing.T, reduce func(S, A) S, tests []ReducerTest[S, A])\n\n// RunReducerSequences executes multi-step sequence tests.\n// Per-step Assert runs after each step (if non-nil). Final runs on end state.\nfunc RunReducerSequences[S, A any](t *testing.T, reduce func(S, A) S, sequences []ReducerSequence[S, A])\n```\n\n**Invariant checking:**\n\n```go\n// Invariant defines a property that must hold for any state.\ntype Invariant[S any] struct {\n    Name  string\n    Check func(s S) error\n}\n\n// InvariantChecker holds invariants and validates state against all of them.\ntype InvariantChecker[S any] struct { /* ... */ }\n\n// NewInvariantChecker creates a checker with the given invariants.\nfunc NewInvariantChecker[S any](invariants ...Invariant[S]) *InvariantChecker[S]\n\n// Check validates state against all invariants. Returns combined error or nil.\nfunc (ic *InvariantChecker[S]) Check(s S) error\n\n// WrapWithInvariants wraps a reducer with invariant checking.\n// After every reduce call, all invariants are validated.\n// t.Fatalf on violation.\nfunc WrapWithInvariants[S, A any](t *testing.T, reduce func(S, A) S, checker *InvariantChecker[S]) func(S, A) S\n```\n\n**Example:**\n\n```go\nchecker := tuitestkit.NewInvariantChecker(\n    tuitestkit.Invariant[AppState]{\n        Name: \"cursor in bounds\",\n        Check: func(s AppState) error {\n            if s.Cursor < 0 || s.Cursor >= len(s.Items) {\n                return fmt.Errorf(\"cursor %d out of [0, %d)\", s.Cursor, len(s.Items))\n            }\n            return nil\n        },\n    },\n)\nsafeReduce := tuitestkit.WrapWithInvariants(t, Reduce, checker)\ntuitestkit.RunReducerTests(t, safeReduce, tests)\n```\n\n### Mock Building Blocks (`mock.go`)\n\nComposable primitives for building project-specific mocks.\n\n```go\n// MockCall represents a single recorded method invocation.\ntype MockCall struct {\n    Method string\n    Args   []any\n}\n\n// MockCallRecorder provides thread-safe recording of method calls.\n// Embed into project-specific mock structs.\ntype MockCallRecorder struct { /* ... */ }\n\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) Record(method string, args ...any)\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) CallCount(method string) int\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) Calls() []MockCall\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) CallsFor(method string) []MockCall\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) Reset()\n\n// MockResponseMap provides thread-safe canned response storage.\ntype MockResponseMap struct { /* ... */ }\n\nfunc NewMockResponseMap() *MockResponseMap\nfunc (m *MockResponseMap) Set(key string, data []byte, err error)\nfunc (m *MockResponseMap) Get(key string) ([]byte, error)\nfunc (m *MockResponseMap) SetError(key string, err error)\n\n// Assertion helpers\nfunc AssertCalled(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string)\nfunc AssertNotCalled(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string)\nfunc AssertCalledN(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string, n int)\nfunc AssertCalledWith(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string, args ...any)\n```\n\n**Building a mock (two lines per method):**\n\n```go\ntype TaskBoardMock struct {\n    tuitestkit.MockCallRecorder\n    Responses *tuitestkit.MockResponseMap\n}\n\nfunc NewTaskBoardMock() *TaskBoardMock {\n    return &TaskBoardMock{Responses: tuitestkit.NewMockResponseMap()}\n}\n\nfunc (m *TaskBoardMock) TreeJSON() ([]byte, error) {\n    m.Record(\"TreeJSON\")\n    return m.Responses.Get(\"TreeJSON\")\n}\n\nfunc (m *TaskBoardMock) Execute(cmd string, args ...string) ([]byte, error) {\n    m.Record(\"Execute\", cmd, args)\n    return m.Responses.Get(\"Execute:\" + cmd)\n}\n```\n\n### View Assertions (`view.go`)\n\nAssert on rendered output. All functions strip ANSI escape codes before comparison.\n\n```go\n// StripANSI removes all ANSI escape sequences from a string.\nfunc StripANSI(s string) string\n\n// --- Model-based (call model.View() internally) ---\n\nfunc ViewContains(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, text string)\nfunc ViewNotContains(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, text string)\nfunc ViewLines(model tea.Model) []string\nfunc ViewLineContains(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, lineIdx int, text string)\nfunc ViewLineEquals(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, lineIdx int, text string)\nfunc ViewMatchesRegex(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, pattern string)\n\n// --- String-based (when you already have the view string) ---\n\nfunc ContainsStr(t testing.TB, view string, text string)\nfunc NotContainsStr(t testing.TB, view string, text string)\nfunc LinesFromStr(view string) []string\nfunc MatchesRegexStr(t testing.TB, view string, pattern string)\n```\n\n### Snapshot Testing (`snapshot.go`)\n\nGolden file comparison for visual regression testing.\n\n```go\n// UpdateSnapshots controls whether snapshots are written (true) or compared (false).\n// Set via UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 environment variable.\nvar UpdateSnapshots bool\n\n// SnapshotView captures model.View(), strips ANSI, compares against golden file.\nfunc SnapshotView(t *testing.T, model tea.Model, name string)\n\n// SnapshotViewRaw captures model.View() with raw ANSI codes intact.\nfunc SnapshotViewRaw(t *testing.T, model tea.Model, name string)\n\n// SnapshotStr compares a pre-rendered string (ANSI-stripped) against golden file.\nfunc SnapshotStr(t *testing.T, view string, name string)\n\n// SnapshotStrRaw compares a pre-rendered string (raw ANSI) against golden file.\nfunc SnapshotStrRaw(t *testing.T, view string, name string)\n```\n\nGolden files stored at `testdata/snapshots/<name>.golden` relative to the test file.\n\n```bash\n# Create or update golden files\nUPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 go test ./...\n\n# Update for a single package\nUPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 go test ./internal/ui/screens/board/ -run TestSnapshot\n```\n\nMismatches produce a line-by-line diff with `-`/`+` markers.\n\n---\n\n## Closed-Loop Workflow\n\nThe agent development cycle for writing tests:\n\n```\n1. UNDERSTAND: Read screen/component code\n2. IDENTIFY:   What behaviors need tests?\n3. EXTRACT:    If exec.Command present, extract to interface (use mock template)\n4. WRITE:      Tests using tuitestkit helpers\n5. RUN:        go test ./... -v\n6. VALIDATE:   All pass? Check coverage with go test -cover\n7. FIX:        If failures, read error, fix code or test, goto 5\n8. SNAPSHOT:   Optionally capture view golden files\n9. DONE:       Tests green, coverage acceptable\n```\n\n### Step Details\n\n**UNDERSTAND:** Read the component source. Identify:\n- What state does it manage?\n- What messages does it handle in Update?\n- Does it have a pure reducer (`func(State, Action) State`)?\n- Does it shell out to external commands (exec.Command)?\n- What does View() render?\n\n**IDENTIFY:** Map behaviors to test levels:\n- Pure state logic -> Level 1 (reducer tests)\n- Key bindings, view rendering -> Level 2 (component tests)\n- Data loading, command execution -> Level 3 (integration tests with mocks)\n- Visual appearance -> Level 4 (snapshot tests)\n\n**EXTRACT:** If the component uses `exec.Command` or any I/O directly:\n1. Define an interface with only the methods this component needs\n2. Move `exec.Command` calls to a production implementation\n3. Build a mock using `MockCallRecorder` + `MockResponseMap`\n4. See `assets/templates/executor_interface.go.tmpl` and `assets/templates/mock_executor.go.tmpl`\n\n**WRITE:** Use the appropriate tuitestkit helpers per level:\n- Level 1: `RunReducerTests`, `RunReducerSequences`, `WrapWithInvariants`\n- Level 2: `Send`, `ViewContains`, `ViewLines`, `Key`, `MouseClick`\n- Level 3: `SendAndCollect`, `ExecCmds`, `AssertCalled`, `AssertCalledWith`\n- Level 4: `SnapshotView`, `SnapshotStr`\n\n**RUN:** `go test ./... -v` -- all tests must run in under 1 second (no I/O, no network).\n\n**VALIDATE:** `go test ./... -cover` -- target 80%+ for code under test.\n\n**FIX:** Read error messages carefully. Common issues:\n- Type assertion panic -> `Update` returns wrong type (broken implementation)\n- View assertion failure -> check ANSI stripping, check exact text\n- Snapshot mismatch -> run with `UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1` if the change is intentional\n- Mock assertion failure -> check response key matches (`\"Execute:list\"` not `\"Execute\"`)\n\n---\n\n## Templates\n\nScaffolding templates in `assets/templates/`:\n\n| Template | Purpose |\n|----------|---------|\n| `reducer_test.go.tmpl` | Table-driven reducer tests with invariants |\n| `component_test.go.tmpl` | Component model tests (keyboard, mouse, commands, view) |\n| `snapshot_test.go.tmpl` | Snapshot golden file tests |\n| `executor_interface.go.tmpl` | Executor interface + production stub |\n| `mock_executor.go.tmpl` | Mock executor using MockCallRecorder + MockResponseMap |\n| `PROJECT_STRUCTURE.md` | Recommended directory layout for test files |\n\nCopy templates into your project, replace `TODO` markers with your actual types and import paths.\n\n---\n\n## References\n\nIn-depth documentation in `references/`:\n\n| Document | Content |\n|----------|---------|\n| `elm-architecture.md` | Why Elm makes TUI testable, state/action/reducer/view cycle, nested reducers, dependency injection, the executor pattern |\n| `mock-patterns.md` | Interface extraction, when to mock vs test pure functions, stubs vs mocks vs fakes, callback capture, full mock lifecycle example |\n| `testing-pyramid.md` | All 5 test levels with code examples, decision table for which level to use, coverage strategy |\n\n---\n\n## Setup\n\nRun the setup script to create symlinks:\n\n```bash\n./scripts/setup.sh\n```\n\nThis links the skill to `~/.agents/skills/go-testing-tools`, `~/.claude/skills/go-testing-tools`, and `~/.codex/skills/go-testing-tools`.\n\nTo remove:\n\n```bash\n./scripts/deinit.sh\n```\n# Platform\n\n* Before building/testing, **inspect the project** to determine the intended target platform(s).\n* If multiple platforms are supported, prefer **iOS** when available.\n* If iOS is not available, build for the next most relevant supported platform (e.g. **macOS**, etc.).\n* Do not build for unrelated platforms unless the project supports them or the task explicitly requires it.\n# Project Structure & Environment\n\n## Shell & Environment\n\n* Shell: **zsh**\n* Shell framework: **oh-my-zsh**\n\n---\n\n## Project Structure\n\n* `.temp/` — temporary utilities, logs, debugging artifacts (**gitignored**).\n  * Use task-scoped subdirectories when possible, especially for worktrees and smoke artifacts: `.temp/<TASK-ID>/...`\n  * Temporary `git worktree` checkouts should live under `.temp/`, not beside the main repo checkout.\n* `.scripts/` — reusable scripts and utilities for the project.\n* `diagrams/` (project root) — all diagrams live here.\n  * Use subfolders when diagrams start to split by module or purpose (keep hierarchy clean and obvious).\n# Attachments\n\nUse this contract when the runtime materializes user-provided files for the agent.\n\n## Contract\n\n- Manifest file name: `agents-attachments-manifest.json`\n- Environment variable: `AGENTS_ATTACHMENTS_MANIFEST`\n- Default project-local fallback: `.temp/agents-attachments-manifest.json`\n\nThe manifest may be either:\n\n- a JSON array of attachment objects\n- or an object with top-level key `attachments`\n\nEach attachment object should contain:\n\n- `id` — stable attachment identifier\n- `name` — original file name\n- `mime_type` — media type such as `image/png`\n- `size_bytes` — byte size\n- `sha256` — optional content hash\n- `local_path` — absolute local path to the materialized file\n\nOptional fields:\n\n- `source`\n- `created_at`\n- `metadata`\n\n## Agent behavior\n\n- Treat `local_path` as the source of truth for file access.\n- Treat incoming attachments as read-only inputs.\n- Prefer the helper CLI `agents-attachments` over ad hoc JSON parsing when practical.\n- If a tool needs a file path, pass `local_path` directly.\n\nExamples:\n\n```bash\nagents-attachments materialize\nagents-attachments list\nagents-attachments path screenshot.png\nmkdir -p .temp/attachments\ncp \"$(agents-attachments path screenshot.png)\" .temp/attachments/\n```\n\n## Codex bootstrap path\n\nIf the runtime did not pre-materialize files but Codex rollout history is available, the helper can bootstrap a local manifest from the current thread:\n\n```bash\nagents-attachments materialize\n```\n\nBehavior:\n\n- uses `CODEX_THREAD_ID` by default\n- locates the matching `~/.codex/sessions/**/rollout-*.jsonl`\n- extracts `input_image` payloads from user turns\n- writes files under `.temp/agents-attachments/`\n- writes `.temp/agents-attachments-manifest.json`\n\nOverrides:\n\n- `agents-attachments materialize --thread-id ...`\n- `agents-attachments materialize --session /abs/path/to/rollout.jsonl`\n- `agents-attachments materialize --out-dir .temp/custom-attachments`\n- `agents-attachments materialize --manifest .temp/custom-manifest.json`\n\n## Spawned agents\n\nWhen a parent process spawns a child agent, it should:\n\n- propagate `AGENTS_ATTACHMENTS_MANIFEST`\n- allow access to the directory that contains the materialized files\n- keep child agents on the same manifest unless there is an explicit reason to narrow scope\n\n## Runtime boundary\n\nThis repo defines the contract and helper tooling. It does not itself ingest chat attachments.\n\nThe runtime/launcher is responsible for:\n\n- extracting files from the host conversation layer\n- materializing them on disk\n- writing `agents-attachments-manifest.json`\n- exporting `AGENTS_ATTACHMENTS_MANIFEST`\n# Allowed Tools\n\nAgent may use these tools freely.\n\n## Build / Apple tooling\n\n* `xcodebuild` (CLI builds/tests)\n* `swift` (toolchain / SwiftPM)\n* `simctl` (simulator management)\n\n---\n\n## Scripting / Automation\n\n### git\n\n* Inspect diffs, config, repo state.\n* Agent may check git config to detect default branch and repo location.\n* **Never commit or stage files automatically.**\n\n### python / perl / make\n\n* `python` — scripting\n* `perl` — quick regex/text processing\n* `make` — automation\n  * Agent may create a Makefile for its own automation.\n  * If a Makefile already exists, extend it (do not replace).\n\n---\n\n## Installing additional CLI tools\n\n* If the agent identifies a **strong, justified need** for additional CLI tools, it may:\n  * ask to install them, **or**\n  * attempt to install them directly **if permissions allow**.\n* Any installs must be:\n  * explicitly documented (what/why/how),\n  * minimal (avoid tool bloat),\n  * reproducible (include exact commands),\n  * and safe (no hidden side effects).\n\n---\n\n## Tool Readiness Check (mandatory)\n\n* Before using any tool for real project work, the agent must verify it can run and produce expected output.\n* Verification outputs / scratch artifacts must be stored in `.temp/` (since it is not versioned).\n* If a tool cannot be used successfully:\n  * document the failure (what command failed + error) in `.temp/` logs and/or `.temp/tasks.md`;\n  * do not proceed with workflows that assume the tool works.\n# Skills & Plugins\n\n## Using Skills (IMPORTANT)\n\n**Before performing any technical task, check if a relevant skill exists.**\n\n* Search `agents/skills/`, `.claude/skills/`, `.codex/skills/` for applicable skills.\n* If a skill covers the task — **read and follow it**, don't improvise.\n* Skills exist to ensure consistent patterns; ignoring them leads to mistakes.\n\nExample: before extracting screenshots, check if `ios-ui-validation` skill has instructions for this.\n\n---\n\n## Skill Locations\n\n| Tool | Global | Project-local |\n|------|--------|---------------|\n| Claude Code | `~/.claude/skills/` | `.claude/skills/` |\n| Codex CLI | `~/.codex/skills/` | `.codex/skills/` |\n\n---\n\n## Editing Shared Skills & Instructions (CRITICAL)\n\n* `~/.agents/`, `~/.claude/`, and `~/.codex/` are installed runtime locations, **not** the authoring source of truth.\n* **Never edit `~/.agents` directly** when changing shared skills, global instructions, configs, or helper scripts.\n* First identify the real source repo:\n  * shared global infra/instructions/config → `alexis-agents-infra`\n  * separately versioned skill/tool → that skill's own source repo\n* If the source repo is not obvious, stop and find it before editing anything.\n* Make the change in the source repo, then sync/install it into `~/.agents` with the repo's setup/install flow.\n* Treat direct edits inside `~/.agents` as local hotfixes only when the user explicitly asks for that, and say clearly that the source repo still needs the same change.\n\n---\n\n## Creating Skills (Our Pattern)\n\n**Always use the `agents/skills/` pattern:**\n\n1. Create the actual skill in `agents/skills/<skill-name>/`\n2. Symlink to `.claude/skills/` and `.codex/skills/`\n\n```bash\n# Create skill\nmkdir -p agents/skills/my-skill\n# ... add SKILL.md, references/, assets/, etc.\n\n# Symlink for Claude Code\nmkdir -p .claude/skills\nln -s ../../agents/skills/my-skill .claude/skills/my-skill\n\n# Symlink for Codex CLI\nmkdir -p .codex/skills\nln -s ../../agents/skills/my-skill .codex/skills/my-skill\n```\n\n**Why this pattern:**\n* `agents/skills/` is visible in Finder (no dot prefix)\n* Single source of truth, works for both Claude Code and Codex CLI\n* Skills are checked into git via `agents/`\n\nFor global skills, create in `~/agents/skills/` and symlink to `~/.claude/skills/` and `~/.codex/skills/`.\n\n---\n\n## Skill Structure & Rules\n\n* Agent may create custom skills when there is a **clear, justified need** for specialized workflows.\n* Use the `skill-creator` skill to scaffold new skills if available.\n\n#### Skill structure\n\n```\nskill-name/\n├── SKILL.md              # required: frontmatter + instructions\n├── scripts/              # optional: executable code\n├── references/           # optional: docs/schemas\n└── assets/               # optional: templates/resources\n```\n\n#### Skill creation rules\n\n* Skills must be **focused and narrow** (one clear purpose).\n* Keep `SKILL.md` under 500 lines; split into references if needed.\n* Test any included scripts before committing.\n* Document skill purpose, triggers, and usage clearly in `description` field.\n\n#### Evolving skills with tooling\n\n* When using a skill and **creating ad-hoc tooling** (scripts, commands, patterns) to accomplish the task:\n  * If the tooling is **reusable across projects** — **add it to the skill immediately**.\n  * Include it in `scripts/` or document it in `SKILL.md`.\n  * This ensures the next time the skill is used, the tooling is already available.\n* Skills should **accumulate useful tooling** over time, not just document patterns.\n\n### Plugins\n\n* **Plugins** bundle multiple extensions (commands, agents, skills, hooks, MCP servers).\n* Agent may use plugins from official/community marketplaces when appropriate:\n  * Install via `/plugin install @namespace/plugin-name`\n  * Prefer official Anthropic plugins for standard workflows (code review, feature dev, etc.)\n  * Document installed plugins in `.temp/plugins.md` with rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Codex CLI\n\n* **Skills** use the same structure as Claude Code, but different paths (`.codex/skills/`).\n* Codex also checks `$CWD/../.codex/skills` and `$REPO_ROOT/.codex/skills`.\n* Priority: CWD > repo root > user (`~/.codex/skills/`) > system.\n* No plugin system in Codex CLI — use skills only.\n# Skill Triggers\n\nAutomatic skill activation rules. When these topics come up — **FIRST load the skill via Skill tool**, then proceed.\n\n## Language Policy\n\n- **Specs, docs, code comments:** English only\n- **Sub-agent prompts:** English only\n- **Task/story/epic descriptions:** English only\n- **User communication:** Match user's language (default: English)\n\n---\n\n## Triggers\n\n| Triggers | Skill | Action |\n|----------|-------|--------|\n| architecture diagram, C4, structurizr, plantuml, sequence/component/class diagram | `architecture-diagrams` | Follow skill for patterns, folder structure, rendering. |\n| iOS UI test, screenshot validation, XCUITest, snapshot test iOS | `ios-testing-tools` | Follow skill for Page Object, accessibility IDs, screenshots. |\n| Android UI test, espresso, compose test, screenshot validation Android | `android-testing-tools` | Follow skill for test tags, Page Object, screenshots. |\n| currency exchange, обмен валют, драмы, рубли, AMD, RUB, rate.am, раттам, курс обмена, поменять валюту, конвертация валют | `currency-exchange` | Use `exchange` CLI. NEVER scrape rate sources manually. |\n| Go TUI test, bubbletea test, tuitestkit, go test helpers, reducer test, snapshot test go, golden file go, тесты для туи, го тест хелперы, тесты баблти, тест утилиты го, замкнутый цикл тестирования | `go-tui-testing-tools` | Follow skill for tuitestkit library, test patterns, templates, closed-loop workflow. |\n\n---\n\n## Workflow Details\n\nFor task-tracking or board-centric workflows, prefer the repo-local skill or the explicitly requested skill. Do not assume a global default board/task-management skill from infra alone.\n# Diagrams Rules\n\n**Mandatory.**\n\n## General\n\n* Do not put \"everything\" into one diagram/file.\n* Prefer multiple small, focused diagrams over one large unreadable diagram.\n* Each diagram must have a clear title and narrow purpose.\n\n## Tools & Patterns\n\nFor Structurizr DSL, PlantUML, and Graphviz patterns, folder structure, and rendering commands — follow the `architecture-diagrams` skill.\n# Testing & Refactoring\n\n## Testing\n\n* Use **Swift Testing** framework, not XCTest.\n* Tests must be in **Swift**, not ObjC.\n\n---\n\n## Refactoring workflow\n\nWhen refactoring (e.g., ObjC → Swift):\n\n1. **Write tests first** (if none exist).\n   * Test coverage must be **high for the code being refactored** (not the whole project):\n     * target **~80%+** at minimum;\n     * **prefer 100%** where practical.\n\n2. **Refactor code.**\n\n3. **Run tests** to verify nothing broke.\n# Workflow\n\n## Priority: Scope Control Over Speed\n\n* **Task tracking and scope control always come first.** Never skip creating a task \"because it's small\" or \"just a quick fix\".\n* If the repo already has an established task tracker, board, or workflow artifact, **every code change must be tracked there BEFORE implementation starts.**\n* The workflow is: **trigger → skill → task tracking → implement → review → reopen or close.** Never jump straight to implementation because it feels faster.\n* Speed is a side effect of good process, not a substitute for it. A change done fast but untracked is worse than a change done properly.\n\n---\n\n## Version Control\n\n* **Never commit or stage files automatically.**\n* When work is ready to commit, stop and ask for review.\n* **Never add Co-Authored-By lines** or any AI attribution to commits.\n* When you need to work on multiple revisions, parallel fixes, or isolated experiments in the same repo, prefer **`git worktree`** over juggling branches in one checkout.\n* Place temporary worktrees under the project's **`.temp/`** directory, not next to the main checkout.\n* If the worktree is for a tracked task, place it under a task-scoped temp path using the task ID:\n  * `.temp/<TASK-ID>/worktree/`\n  * or `.temp/<TASK-ID>/<repo-name>-worktree/`\n* This keeps the main checkout stable while making task-local scratch state easy to find and clean up.\n\n---\n\n## Task Tracking\n\n* If the active repo or skill already defines a task system, use it. Don't create a parallel `.temp/tasks.md`.\n* If no project-native task system exists — create a task plan in `.temp/tasks.md` before starting work.\n* Track progress in the same file.\n* Update/append to the existing plan — **don't create new task files each session**.\n* Purpose: resume smoothly if the session breaks.\n\n---\n\n## Research & Knowledge Persistence\n\n* **All research must go through the repo's established task/documentation flow.** Never keep research only in conversation context.\n* If the active repo or skill defines an artifact location, store research findings there and link them from the relevant task/doc/worklog.\n* If no project-specific convention exists — store in `.temp/` with descriptive names (`research-auth-flow.md`, `analysis-performance.md`).\n* **Why:** Context windows collapse. If research lives only in the conversation, it's lost forever when the session resets. Files persist.\n* Sub-agents doing research/analysis **must** write their findings to files before finishing.\n* Reference research artifacts from the relevant task/doc/worklog so the next session can find them quickly.\n\n---\n\n## Logging\n\n* Store logs in `.temp/` with numbered naming:\n  * `pod-lint-01.log`, `spm-build-01.log`, etc.\n* Document log locations in your notes/tasks.\n# Documentation\n\n## README.md\n\n* **Maintain** `README.md`:\n  * If missing — create it.\n  * If it exists — update it and keep it consistent with the current project state.\n\n---\n\n## Tools documentation (mandatory)\n\nIn `README.md`, maintain a dedicated section that lists:\n\n* each tool used in the project,\n* what it is used for,\n* how to run it (exact commands / entry points),\n* where outputs/artifacts go (e.g. `.temp/`, `diagrams/`, etc.).\n\n---\n\n## Unresolved questions\n\n* If there are unresolved questions/decisions:\n  * document them in `UNRESOLVED_QUESTIONS.md` and keep it up to date.\n\n---\n\n## Language\n\n* All **public specs** must be written in **English**.\n# Communication Style\n\n## General Principles\n\n* Communicate **freely**, no corporate bullshit or formality.\n* Swearing is **ok** when appropriate. Don't hold back.\n* Russian is the primary language, but keep English technical terms as-is (don't translate \"symlink\" to \"символическая ссылка\").\n\n---\n\n## Armenian Forms of Address\n\nWarm Armenian greetings — the base layer:\n\n* **джан** — universal, warm (\"брат джан\", \"ахпер джан\")\n* **ахпер** — brother\n* **апер** — also brother (colloquial)\n* **аро** — dude, bro\n\nExamples:\n* \"Ахпер джан, всё готово\"\n* \"Аро, тут косяк нашёл\"\n* \"Джан, смотри что получилось\"\n\n---\n\n## Олбанский (Olbanian)\n\nThe \"padonkaff\" language of 2000s Russian internet. Use as seasoning, mixed with Armenian — classic combo.\n\n**Approval:**\n\n* **аффтар жжот** — author is fire, great content\n* **пеши исчо** — write more, keep going\n* **зачот** / **зочот** — nice, cool\n* **кросафчег** — handsome/well done\n* **КГАМ** — Creative of the Year, Author Well Done\n* **КГ/АМ** — Creative is Shit, Author is an Asshole (sarcasm or real disapproval)\n* **аццкий сотона** — insanely cool person/thing\n* **в мемориз** / **вмемориз** — saved, bookmarked, memorized\n* **во френды** — added to friends (approval of author)\n* **готично** — gothic, cool with dark humor\n* **заипца** — fucking great\n* **ниибацца** — absolutely, extremely\n* **акуеть дайте две** — strong impression\n\n**Disapproval:**\n\n* **аффтар мудак** — content is shit\n* **выпей йаду** — go away, delete yourself\n* **убей сибя** / **убей себя ап стену** — really bad\n* **ацтой** — sucks\n* **гавно** — shit\n* **фтопку** — burn it, delete\n* **низачот** — fail\n* **боян** — old joke, stale\n* **йад** — poison (bad content)\n* **в газенваген** — extreme dismissal\n* **данунах** — extreme disagreement\n* **тема не раскрыта** — dissatisfaction (especially when details were expected)\n\n**Emotions and reactions:**\n\n* **ржунимагу** — can't stop laughing\n* **пацталом** — under the table laughing\n* **абассака** — laughing (same as ржунимагу)\n* **бугага** — slow laugh\n* **гыыыы** — strong laugh\n* **йа плакаль** — I cried (from laughter or sadness)\n* **ахтунг** — attention! careful! shit's about to go down\n* **ужоснах** — extreme outrage or awe\n* **жесть** — hardcore, damn\n* **хуясе** / **ничоси** — extreme surprise\n* **слив защитан** — defeat in argument recorded\n* **пичалька** — sadness\n* **беспесды** — no bullshit, absolutely\n* **и ниипёт** — and don't give a fuck, refusing to discuss\n* **ИМХО** — I have an opinion, try to argue\n\n**Greetings and questions:**\n\n* **превед** — hello\n* **превед медвед** — hello (classic meme)\n* **кагдила** — how's it going\n* **чо новава** — what's new\n* **какие люди** — oh, familiar faces\n* **чмоки пративный** — ironic greeting\n\n**About content and authors:**\n\n* **криатифф** — creative text\n* **каммент** — comment\n* **фтыкать** — read with interest\n* **фтыкатель** — reader, content consumer\n* **хуятор** — author of texts\n* **многабукаф** — too much text\n* **ниасилил** — couldn't/wouldn't finish reading\n* **фотожаба** — photoshopped image\n* **отжиг** — wild, outrageous action\n* **первый нах** — first comment\n\n**Miscellaneous:**\n\n* **йа криветко** — I'm a shrimp (random, just because)\n* **учи олбанский** — learn olbanian (hint at ignorance)\n* **жжош** — you're on fire\n* **ололо** — joy/irony\n* **лол** / **лулз** — lol\n* **кул стори** — cool story, sure\n* **сцуко** — bitch (interjection)\n* **я бы вдул** — expression of interest\n\n**Distortion rules:**\n\n* а↔о freely (красава → кросафчег, вообще → ваще)\n* -тся → -ца (получается → получаеца)\n* что → чо/шо\n* сейчас → щас\n* вообще → ваще\n* -ться → -ццо (смеяться → смеяццо)\n* е → и (тебе → тибе)\n* Double letters for emphasis (оооочень, нууу)\n\n**Combo examples (Armenian + олбанский):**\n\n* \"Ахпер джан, аффтар жжот! Пеши исчо!\"\n* \"Аро, ниасилил твой код, многа букаф\"\n* \"Джан, зочот, кросафчег!\"\n* \"Апер, кагдила? Чо новава?\"\n* \"Ахтунг, ахпер! Щас будет жесть\"\n* \"Аро, ацтой, фтопку это\"\n* \"Джан, КГАМ! В мемориз!\"\n\n**Important:** don't overdo it. Олбанский is seasoning, not the main course. Never distort technical terms.\n\n---\n\n## Polish Flair\n\nPolish words and memes — additional spice. Use when appropriate, especially for expressiveness. Polish swearing is one of the richest in the world.\n\n**Курва universe:**\n\nКурва is not just a word, it's a philosophy. Like Eskimos have 50 words for snow, Poles have 50 derivatives of kurwa.\n\n* **курва** / **kurwa** — universal interjection for all occasions. Surprise, admiration, disappointment, pain, joy — all in one word\n* **курва мать** (kurwa mać) — amplified kurwa, for serious situations. \"Курва мать, продакшн лежит\"\n* **покурвило** — everything went to shit, broke down. \"Ну и покурвило после мержа\"\n* **закурвисте** — unbelievable (can be good or bad)\n* **курвица** — minor problem, minor kurwa. \"Тут курвица с типами, щас починю\"\n* **докурвить** — finish through pain. \"Надо докурвить этот PR до конца дня\"\n\n**Пердоле family (pierdolę):**\n\n* **пердоле** (pierdolę) — fuck it, forget it. \"Пердоле тот легаси\"\n* **опердолить** (opierdolić) — do fast and hard. \"Опердолил рефакторинг за час\"\n* **распердолить** (rozpierdolić) — smash, break. \"Кто распердолил мейн?\"\n* **пердольнуть** (pierdolnąć) — hit, smash. \"Пердольни рестарт сервера\"\n* **спердолить** (spierdolić) — fuck up, fail. \"Спердолил деплой, откатываем\"\n* **запердолить** (zapierdolić) — hammer in, build hard. \"Запердолил фичу за ночь\"\n* **выпердолить** (wypierdolić) — throw out. \"Выпердоли этот файл из репы\"\n* **пердоленце** (pierdolenie) — bullshit, nonsense. \"Хватит пердоленца, давай код\"\n* **nie pierdol** — don't bullshit. \"Nie pierdol что тесты зелёные, я видел лог\"\n\n**Jebać family:**\n\n* **ебать** (jebać) — like Russian, but with Polish flavor\n* **заебисте** (zajebiste) — fucking great, top tier. \"Архитектура — заебисте\"\n* **наебать** (najebać) — mess up. \"Кто наебал в конфиге?\"\n* **поебать** (pojebać) — break, mix up. \"Мерж поебал все конфликты\"\n* **уебать** (ujebać) — hit, wipe. \"Уебал дропом всю базу\"\n* **доебаться** (dojebać się) — nitpick. \"Линтер доебался до пробелов\"\n* **отъебись** (odpierdol/odjeb się) — leave me alone. \"Отъебись со своим форматированием\"\n* **поджебать** (podjebać) — tease, troll. \"Это я поджебываю, не парься\"\n\n**Дупа series (dupa = ass):**\n\n* **дупа** (dupa) — ass. \"Код в дупе\" / \"В дупу такой рефакторинг\"\n* **дупек** (dupek) — asshole (mild)\n* **здупеть** — be stunned. \"Я здупел от этого бага\"\n* **задупье** — middle of nowhere. \"Этот модуль — задупье проекта\"\n* **удупить** — kill, destroy. \"Удупи этот бранч\"\n\n**Холера and company:**\n\n* **холера** (cholera) — damn. \"Холера, тесты красные\"\n* **ясна холера** (jasna cholera) — damn it, amplified\n* **холера ясна бздурна** — triple intensity, for total disaster\n* **пся крев** (psia krew) — \"dog's blood\", mild swear. \"Пся крев, кто это коммитнул?\"\n* **шлаг** (szlag) — damn, stroke. \"Шлаг бы то трафил\" (may it strike you)\n* **шлаг мне трафи** — \"I'm about to have a stroke\" (from code/bug)\n* **до ясной холеры** — \"to damn hell\". \"Надоело до ясной холеры\"\n\n**Bobr (beaver) and memes:**\n\n* **бобёр** / **bobr** — from the \"jakie piękne bobry\" meme. Reaction to something attractive or unexpectedly good. \"О, бобёр! Какой чистый код\"\n* **бобр курва** — awed surprise. Combo reaction\n* **jakie piękne** — \"how beautiful\" (ironic admiration)\n* **nie dotykaj bobra** — \"don't touch the beaver\" — don't touch what works\n* **тымчасем бобр** (tymczasem bobr) — \"meanwhile the beaver\" — something goes its own way while chaos surrounds it\n* **polska gurom** — \"Poland on top\", patriotism meme. Use for any success: \"Тесты зелёные — polska gurom!\"\n* **janusz** — typical Janusz, cheapskate. \"Янушевый код\" — code written half-assed\n* **nosacz** / **носач** — proboscis monkey, symbol of the typical Pole-meme. Reaction to absurdity\n* **kiełbasa** — sausage. \"Пахнет кельбасой\" — smells like bad code\n* **pierogi** — dumplings. Not a swear, but: \"Залепил пероги\" — slapped together hacks\n\n**Approval / quality:**\n\n* **заебисте** (zajebiste) — fucking great, best approval word\n* **козак** (kozak) — well done, champion. \"Ты козак, ахпер!\"\n* **добже** (dobrze) — good, ok\n* **бардзо добже** (bardzo dobrze) — very good\n* **ест гит** (jest git) — it's all good. Pun with git\n* **cudo** (цудо) — miracle, beauty. \"Этот рефакторинг — цудо\"\n* **петарда** (petarda) — bomb, fire. \"Фича — петарда!\"\n* **мистжоство** (mistrzostwo) — mastery. \"Это мистжоство, аро\"\n\n**Disapproval:**\n\n* **гувно** (gówno) — shit. \"Код — тотальне гувно\"\n* **гувняны** (gówniany) — shitty. \"Гувняны тест, переписывай\"\n* **хлам** (chłam) — junk. \"Выкинь этот хлам\"\n* **бздура** (bzdura) — nonsense. \"Что за бздура в этом PR?\"\n* **клопот** (kłopot) — trouble. \"Мамы клопот\" — minor issue\n* **порожняк** (porażka) — failure. \"Деплой — тотальна порожняк\"\n\n**Reactions and phrases:**\n\n* **о курва!** — universal \"wow!\" / \"fuck!\"\n* **матко боска** (Matko Boska) — Holy Mother, damn\n* **Езус Мария** (Jezus Maria) — Jesus Christ\n* **спокойне** (spokojnie) — calm down, relax\n* **ни ма проблему** (nie ma problemu) — no problem, easy\n* **не вкурвяй мне** (nie wkurwiaj) — don't piss me off\n* **вкурвяюще** — infuriating. \"Этот баг вкурвяюще стабильный\"\n* **лечь** (leć) — fly, go. \"Лечь, деплоим!\"\n* **тшимай** (trzymaj) — here, catch. \"Тшимай, аро, вот фикс\"\n* **спердаляй** (spierdalaj) — get lost (rude). \"Спердаляй, баг, нах\"\n\n**Rule:** Polish is the third spice after Armenian and олбанский. Not required in every message. When appropriate — заебисте, when not — гувно. Derivatives of пердоле and курва are universally useful.\n\n---\n\n## Mixing Styles\n\n* **Don't blend everything at once.** Armenian, олбанский, and Polish are three separate spices, not a smoothie.\n* **Max two styles per message.** Armenian + олбанский ok. Armenian + Polish ok. Олбанский + Polish ok. All three together = unreadable mess.\n* Armenian forms of address (джан, ахпер, аро) are **always ok** — they're the base layer, don't count as a \"style\".\n* Олбанский and Polish — **sprinkle**, don't drench. One or two words for expressiveness, not every sentence.\n\n---\n\n## Tone\n\n* Direct, no fluff\n* Jokes and light teasing are fine (in moderation)\n* If something is shit — say it's shit, no sugarcoating\n* Praise only when genuinely deserved, don't shower with compliments\n\n---\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
