{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '401,800p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"## File Formats\n\nEach element directory contains two files:\n- **README.md** — static description (what the element is, scope, acceptance criteria). Does not change during execution.\n- **progress.md** — dynamic lifecycle journal (status, Task/Bug estimate, assignment, timestamps, dependencies, checklist, notes). Updated throughout execution.\n\nBoth files use source-aware, injection-safe Markdown persistence. Unchanged\nbytes and custom sections are retained during rewrites; free-text payload fields\nround-trip byte-exact. Mutation boundaries normalize identifier-like fields\n(`AssignedTo`, `BlockedBy`, `Blocks`) by trimming boundary whitespace and reject\nempty or reserved `(none)` values with a typed error. Checklist text is rejected\nonly when empty-after-trim and is otherwise persisted verbatim.\n\nTask/bug dependencies are bidirectional ground truth. Story/epic ordering is derived from the live task DAG at read time; task links never materialize container side-effect links. Explicit stored container links remain manual state, while projections expose task-derived blockers separately.\n\nFor detailed format specifications, field descriptions, and examples, see [`references/file-formats.md`](references/file-formats.md).\n\n---\n\n## Resources & Bugs\n\nResources are linked to board elements via DSL mutations. Two types:\n- **Precondition** — inputs for the task (instructions, specs, design docs). Used by `task-board spawn` to compose agent prompts.\n- **Outcome** — work products (research results, implementation notes). Persisted for other agents/tasks to consume.\n\n```bash\n# Create resource\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md, content=\"...\", type=precondition, description=\"Architecture design\")'\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_results.md, content=\"...\", type=outcome, description=\"Research findings\")'\n\n# Replace existing resource\ntask-board m 'update_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_results.md, content=\"revised findings\", type=outcome, description=\"Research findings v2\")'\n\n# Delete resource\ntask-board m --confirm 'delete_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md)'\n```\n\nResources are stored flat in `.task-board/.resources/<elementID>/` and referenced from `progress.md`. Bugs live inside stories alongside tasks.\n\nFor resource workflow and bug tracking — see [`references/resources-and-bugs.md`](references/resources-and-bugs.md).\n\n---\n\n## Workflow\n\n### Full Development Cycle (MANDATORY)\n\nWhen the user asks to build/implement something, the agent MUST follow this end-to-end flow:\n\n#### Spec\n- Specs live in the `.spec/` directory (one or more `.md` files), not in a single `SPEC.md`\n- Create `.spec/` if it doesn't exist; add focused spec files per topic/feature (e.g. `.spec/auth.md`, `.spec/api.md`)\n- Clarify requirements with the user if anything is ambiguous\n- `.spec/` is the source of truth — all work traces back to it\n\n#### Plan on the Board\n- Create epics and stories: `task-board m 'create(type=epic, name=..., description=\"...\")'`\n- Refine each element: query with `task-board q 'get(ID) { full }'`, clarify gaps, update via `task-board m 'set_details(ID, description=\"...\")'`\n- Perform spec-to-board decomposition inline as the Orchestrator; do not create a meta-task or spawn `solution-architect` merely to lay out a settled spec\n- No element should have placeholder text — description and AC must be complete and unambiguous\n\n#### Story Detailing, Phase Planning, Execution (MANDATORY)\n\nAfter the Orchestrator completes story detailing inline, use [`references/planning-workflow.md`](references/planning-workflow.md) for phase planning, user approval, pre-launch checks, specialist execution, supervision, and visualization. Its legacy decomposition-spawn examples do not override this inline mandate and must not be used for settled-spec board layout.\n\nKey points:\n- **Story detailing before planning** — the Orchestrator creates and refines the required stories and tasks inline before phase planning\n- **No meta-delegated board layout** — do not create a meta-task or spawn `solution-architect` solely to translate a settled spec into board elements; reserve that role for genuine architecture decisions such as system-design tradeoffs or unresolved cross-module contracts\n- **Use `task-board q 'plan(...)'` as the only planning source** — default JSON returns nested phases and `criticalPath`; compact output returns flat `waves` and a single-line `criticalPath`. Unresolvable dependencies fail with a typed validation error instead of producing misleading phases. Agents never need a rendered graph to plan. Use `task-board plan --save` only to persist a human-readable snapshot; never build phases manually or write plan files by hand\n- **Dump plan to `.planning/`** — after planning, save to `.planning/{YYMMDD}_{HHmmss}_{topic}.md` for session recovery. Use `task-board plan --save`\n- **User approval MANDATORY** — never start execution without user confirmation\n- **Pre-launch checklist** — model, status, assignment, concrete IDs, first command\n- **Coordinator is SUPERVISOR** — never writes code, only delegates to sub-agents\n\n#### Research / Analysis Tasks\n\nResearch workflow, resource linking, document structure, status flow — see [`references/research-workflow.md`](references/research-workflow.md).\n\nKey points:\n- Research results are linked to board elements via `task-board m 'add_resource(..., type=outcome)'` on first write and `update_resource(...)` on revisions\n- Broadly useful research also persists in **`.research/`** directory (project-level)\n- Status flow: `backlog → analysis → to-review → reviewing → done` (skip `development`; after `to-review`, spawn an explicit reviewer such as `task-board spawn TASK-... --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`)\n- Always link research output as an outcome resource on the task, and include the task ID in the resource name\n\n### Iterative Refinement (for individual elements)\n\nWhen creating ANY element (epic/story/task/bug), the agent MUST:\n\n1. **Create** element via `task-board m 'create(type=..., name=..., description=\"...\")'`\n2. **Read** the element: `task-board q 'get(ID) { full }'`\n3. **Clarify** — if description is vague or has gaps, ask the user\n4. **Update** via `task-board m 'set_details(ID, description=\"...\", ac=\"...\")'`\n5. **Repeat** steps 2-4 until description is exhaustive\n6. Element is **ready** only when description and AC are complete and unambiguous\n\n### Project Start\n\n- Create elements: `task-board m 'create(type=epic, name=auth-system, description=\"...\")'`\n- Create stories: `task-board m 'create(type=story, parent=EPIC-260218-abc, name=login)'`\n- Detail tasks: `task-board m 'create(type=task, parent=STORY-260218-abc, name=impl)'`\n- Set dependencies: `task-board m 'link(STORY-260218-def, blocked_by=STORY-260218-abc)'`\n- **Generate phases**: `task-board q 'plan(EPIC-260218-abc, mode=children)'` — only after all stories have tasks and dependencies are set\n\n### Spawning Agents for Tasks\n\nThe **coordinator never writes code** — it delegates through tracked spawn commands with explicit role/model selection and runtime-affine agent selection.\n\nTreat [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) as the canonical operator-facing spawn contract. The guidance below is the workflow summary agents should follow.\n\n**`--role`, `--background`, and `--model` are REQUIRED.** Codex and Qwen spawns also require `--reasoning-effort`; Claude spawns do not accept it. Choose the role based on the task:\n\n`--agent` is optional by design. Canonical `spawn.preferred_agentic_system` is an enforced tagged union: `exclusive` pins one provider; `mixed` permits a normalized provider set and retains runtime affinity when it belongs to that set. An explicit `--agent` wins only within the configured set; an out-of-set explicit provider or affinity is refused before task lookup or spawn side effects and names the allowed providers. When the canonical system is absent, legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` retains its ceiling/executable-gated preference and affinity fallback; when both are absent, a Codex orchestrator session spawns a `codex` child and a Claude orchestrator session spawns a `claude` child. The implementation detects Codex from `CODEX_SESSION`, `CODEX_THREAD_ID`, or `CODEX_CI` and otherwise uses Claude.\n\nOptional `spawn.schedule.windows[]` overlays that base provider system during disjoint UTC windows. Each window is start-inclusive/end-exclusive, uses `HH:MM`, may cross midnight, and carries its own `preferred_agentic_system`; equal or overlapping bounds are invalid. The active window replaces the base system before provider and registered-model validation, while gaps use the base system or unrestricted runtime affinity when no base exists. Inspect `project_config().spawn.schedule` for the configured windows, `active_window`, and `base_preferred_agentic_system` before selecting a provider.\n\n### Goal-Capable Primary Sessions\n\nThe fixed primary-session UX is `agents-infra codex` for Codex and\n`agents-infra claude` for Claude. Both are fully capable of starting\ngoal-bound tracked owners through `task-board spawn --goal-scope`.\n\n- `agents-infra` remains board-agnostic: it has no `task-board` dependency and\n  does not create, discover, or preserve a caller goal plane.\n- `task-board codex` is an optional enhanced Codex primary session. It is never\n  required to own a goal-bound tracked run.\n- `CODEX_THREAD_ID`, `CODEX_SESSION`, and `CODEX_CI` select Codex affinity only;\n  they do not identify an endpoint or form an addressable caller goal plane.\n\n| Capability | `agents-infra codex` primary | `task-board codex` optional primary | `agents-infra claude` primary |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Launch the initial orchestrator | Required Codex UX | Optional, not required UX | Required Claude UX |\n| Start a goal-bound tracked owner | Yes | Yes | Yes |\n| Bind the native goal before owner work | Dedicated Codex app-server/thread | Dedicated Codex app-server/thread | Fresh Claude process with native `/goal` |\n| Preserve an existing caller goal | Zero caller RPCs when plane is absent | Exact snapshot/restore when plane is reachable | Not applicable; no caller-session rewrite |\n| Query/upsert active goal, directives, acceptance, and recovery | Yes | Yes | Yes |\n| Durable `events`, `watch`, and `wait` observation | Yes | Yes | Yes |\n| Push terminal state into an active Codex thread | No | Yes | No Codex push; Claude harness background wait wakes the session |\n| Codex auto-continue nudges | No | Yes | Not a Codex capability |\n| Caller-session app-server RPC access | No supported endpoint | Yes | Not applicable |\n\nParity is behavioral, not mechanical: both providers create a fresh tracked\nowner, bind a native continuation condition before delivery work, and share the\nsame immutable revision, scope, directive, recovery, and acceptance contract.\nCodex uses a dedicated app-server thread; Claude uses a fresh `/goal` process.\nSee [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md)\nfor the operator contract.\n\n| Task type | Role |\n|-----------|------|\n| Research, analysis, investigation | `--role researcher` |\n| Code implementation, bugfixes | `--role developer` |\n| Writing tests | `--role tester` |\n| Genuine architecture decisions: system-design tradeoffs or unresolved cross-module contracts | `--role solution-architect` |\n| Documentation | `--role doc-writer` |\n| Code review, quality check | `--role reviewer` |\n\n**Workflow:**\n\n1. **Preflight runtime** — run `task-board q 'project_config()'` before spawning so you know whether this cwd is using a local board or a remote profile and whether `spawn.enabled` permits launches. If it is `false`, do not attempt a spawn or a flag-based workaround; the project policy must be changed first.\n2. **Resolve the agent, then select deliberately for quality per token** — inspect `project_config().spawn.preferred_agentic_system` and the active `spawn.schedule` window. Honor an explicit human/task-context request with `--agent` only when it belongs to the effective set; otherwise omit it so `exclusive` pins its provider or `mixed` retains an in-set runtime affinity. Legacy `preferred_agent` remains a best-effort compatibility path. For the selected agent, assess scope, complexity, risk, required autonomy, validation burden, and expected context and token cost; inspect `task-board q --format compact 'models(agent=<selected-agent>)'`; then choose the strongest suitable explicit model and compatible reasoning effort for Codex or Qwen. Task-board supplies no model or reasoning defaults. A configured `model_criterion: equal` resolves every explicit request to that selected agent's configured model without relying on model ranks.\n3. **Prepare task** — ensure element has clear title, description, scope, AC\n4. **Add task-specific DoD on the board** — checklist items on the element are the task-specific DoD that spawn validates before launch\n   ```bash\n   task-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-260218-abc, text=\"Handle edge case X\")'\n   task-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-260218-abc, text=\"Preserve existing API response shape\")'\n   ```\n5. **Link instruction** (optional) — add precondition resource:\n   ```bash\n   # Inline text instruction\n   task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=TASK-260218-abc_impl-guide.md, content=\"...\", type=precondition, description=\"Implementation guide\")'\n\n   # File or URL attachment\n   task-board resource add TASK-260218-abc ./TASK-260218-abc_impl-guide.md --type precondition\n\n   # Chat attachment only (no local path yet) — requires relux-agents-infra:\n   # https://github.com/relux-works/relux-agents-infra\n   agents-attachments materialize\n   task-board resource add TASK-260218-abc \"$(agents-attachments path attachment-001)\" --name TASK-260218-abc_attachment-001.bin --type precondition\n   ```\n6. **Spawn with the effective provider system, affinity, or an in-set explicit override** — after reading `project_config()`, omit `--agent` when `exclusive` should pin its provider or `mixed` contains the current affinity. Pass `--agent` for a human/task-context provider only when the effective set allows it. With no canonical system or legacy preference, `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max` follows runtime affinity and auto-composes the prompt from role + README + checklist DoD + precondition resources\n   - From a Claude-affine orchestrator with no preference, the equivalent shape is `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model claude-opus-4-8`\n   - With instruction validation: `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max --instruction TASK-260218-abc_impl-guide.md`\n   - Explicit override from either runtime: `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role reviewer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-4-8`\n   - Before the first producer launch or delivery status mutation for an approved pool, start its tracked owner: `task-board spawn STORY-260218-auth --role orchestrator --goal-scope TASK-260218-a,TASK-260218-b --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`\n   - A goal-bound Codex owner runs in a dedicated app-server thread created by its tracked runner, regardless of whether the primary session came from `agents-infra codex` or `task-board codex`. The goal is bound there before the first turn. If no task-board caller endpoint is declared, preflight persists `caller_goal_plane=absent` and performs zero caller RPCs; a complete reachable declaration uses the exact snapshot/restore transaction. Partial or unreachable declarations fail closed before spawn side effects. Nested producer/reviewer launches therefore cannot replace the parent delivery condition. Do not replace this with an ordinary prompt-only `codex exec` launch.\n   - A goal-bound child must remain inside the latest active scope. On typed `goal_scope_mismatch`, run the exact revision-bound `task-board spawn goal upsert \"$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID\" --if-revision ... --add-scope ... --reason ...` remediation and retry; never silently absorb work.\n   - A declared parent run context never downgrades silently. While Claude `successor_required` is pending, nested spawn fails side-effect-free with `goal_provider_binding_pending`; if the declared run cannot be loaded, it fails as `goal_parent_context_unavailable`. Retry only from the active rebound successor context.\n7. **Capture the run id** — tracked background spawn returns `RUN-...`; use it as the coordinator handle for that child execution\n   - CLI output now prints non-blocking monitor commands with a seeded cursor (`RUN-ID:0`) and preserves the active `task-board` executable / board context\n   - JSON output exposes the same self-contained contract under `observation` (`cursor`, `eventsCommand`, `watchCommand`, `waitCommand`, `runtime`, `delivery`, `inSessionDelivery`)\n8. **Monitor** — `task-board spawn status RUN-...`, `task-board spawn events RUN-... --cursor ...`, `task-board spawn watch RUN-...`, `task-board spawn wait RUN-...`, and `task-board q --format compact 'agents()'`\n   - Directive plane: `task-board spawn directives RUN-...` to inspect durable operator directives and `task-board spawn directive RUN-... <kind>` to request `cancel`, `pause_after_checkpoint`, `restart`, `reroute_after_checkpoint`, `request_progress`, or `nudge`\n   - Goal plane: `task-board spawn goal RUN-...` before nested spawn, at directive checkpoints, and before success claims. It returns the current objective, resolved leaf scope, and optimistic revision.\n   - Owner-agent upsert exposes no goal kind or predicate flags. Creation uses `--scope`; updates require `--if-revision` and use only `--add-scope` and/or `--objective-intent`, with a reason for every non-idempotent revision. The mutation response includes `previous_revision`: `0` for creation, the replaced active revision for a change, and the current revision for an idempotent replay.\n   - If Claude reports `successor_required` for a goal created by an already-running unbound owner, stop normally at the next safe checkpoint. The tracked runner creates and starts the rebound native-`/goal` successor automatically; the predecessor is superseded, never accepted delivery, and cannot launch nested work while rebinding is pending.\n   - Restart and same-semantics reroute are owner-agent recovery actions that retain the active immutable revision. Operator-only cancellation requires a non-empty reason. Structural reroute uses `reroute_after_checkpoint --goal-scope ... --goal-kind ... --goal-success-predicate ... --goal-objective-intent ... --payload ...`; role compatibility is validated before directive persistence.\n9. **Review via child run** — when a producer sets `to-review`, immediately spawn and monitor a reviewer with explicit model selection; never hand routine review to the human\n10. **Own rework through acceptance** — on changes requested, preserve the verdict as rework context, spawn the appropriate producer, wait for a new `to-review`, and spawn another reviewer. Repeat until accepted `done`\n11. **Recover early exits and parked states** — inspect run events and outcome evidence, then retry or reroute through a focused child; mark `blocked` only when the evidence proves a genuine stop-the-line boundary\n12. **Escalate only a real boundary** — ask the human only for a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision; first record the evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact decision or external input needed\n\nSpawn is task-centric:\n- Primary inputs are task ID + role\n- `task-board q 'project_config()'` is the supported preflight check before spawning or auditing\n- Canonical provider selection is enforced as `preferred_agentic_system`: explicit in-set `--agent`, otherwise exclusive pin or in-set mixed affinity. Without it, legacy usable `preferred_agent` and then runtime affinity retain compatibility.\n- A legacy preference with no provider ceiling or unavailable executable falls back to affinity without hard failure; response and manifest selection evidence record `agentSelectionPath` / `agent_selection_path`, preferred agent, and fallback reason\n- An explicit human or task-context agent request must be passed as `--agent`; it wins inside the canonical allow-set and is refused with typed allowed-provider evidence when outside it\n- Per-agent ceilings are applied only after the agent is selected. Role identity is resolved before ceiling lookup: boundary whitespace and case are normalized, legacy `coordinator` shares the `orchestrator` policy identity, and path-like or unknown roles fail closed with typed errors. Affinity or explicit `--agent` picks the provider; `spawn.ceilings.<agent>` and any role override pin or constrain the model/reasoning fields for that provider and never switch providers\n- Model selection is always explicit; Codex and Qwen reasoning effort is always explicit and must be supported by the selected model\n- `spawn.enabled` defaults to `true`; `false` rejects every spawn before board lookup, task mutation, prompt/manifest creation, or runner launch\n- `spawn.launch_composition.enabled` also defaults to `true`; explicit `false` skips project MCP composition, while unavailable/old/failing/invalid infra keeps the existing bare child launch and records sanitized degradation evidence\n- launch composition is MCP-only. It must not import primary-session model, reasoning, profile, approval, sandbox, service tier, prompt, or goal policy. Bearer values stay in the inherited process environment; only referenced environment-variable names are safe evidence\n- `spawn.ceilings.<agent>.model_criterion: equal` means the configured model is exact, not a ranked upper bound. It requires no selection assessment and does not accept `--selection-rationale` or `--ack-resolved`.\n- `model_criterion: less_or_equal` clamps requests above the configured provider-local `policyRank`; `greater_or_equal` raises requests below it. Reasoning effort follows the same bound direction using the selected provider's ordered vocabulary: Codex supports `low < medium < high < xhigh < max < ultra`, while Qwen supports `low < medium < high < xhigh < max`.\n- Every ordered-criterion spawn requires `--selection-rationale` with one non-empty line explaining the deliberate pair. Task-board records rationale as `(pair_it_justifies, text)` tuples scoped to the task and spawn role. Pass-through and accepted adjusted tuples are stored in the run manifest and task notes; the first adjusted invocation records the requested-pair tuple in task notes before returning the acknowledgement checkpoint.\n- An ordered pass-through needs no acknowledgement. If model or effort changes, task-board reports `requested -> resolved`, records the initial rationale against the requested pair, and stops without assignment, prompt/run creation, or runner launch. Reassess the resolved pair against scope, risk, autonomy, and validation burden; re-request within bounds or escalate if inadequate. Otherwise re-invoke with exact `--ack-resolved <model>/<effort>` (Claude uses the model ID alone) and a fresh one-line rationale for the resolved pair. After whitespace normalization, its text must differ byte-for-byte from every rationale already recorded for that task and role; the CLI binds pairs structurally and does not infer rationale semantics.\n- Task description comes from the board README\n- Task-specific DoD comes from the board checklist\n- Role baseline DoD is appended after task-specific DoD\n- Precondition resources carry additional instructions, screenshots, specs, and artifacts\n- Hidden legacy `--task-path` and `--dod-path` flags are compatibility-only appenders; they do not replace the board README/checklist workflow and should not be part of the normal contract\n- Spawn returns a tracked `RUN-...` handle; use `task-board spawn wait RUN-...` only after a run exists and the parent needs an explicit synchronization barrier\n- Standalone `spawn wait` and fused `spawn --wait` exit zero only for `completed`; `failed` and `cancelled` runs return non-zero, while PID-aware stale repair prevents orphaned queued/running/paused runs from hanging wait/follow observers\n- Logwork files and system log resources are scoped by task plus `RUN-...`, preserving concurrent same-role run evidence independently\n- Spawn output includes coordinator observation hints for non-blocking monitoring (`watch`, `events --cursor RUN-ID:0`, `wait`) and keeps them runnable in the same executable / board context that produced the run\n- `task-board spawn --json` exposes the same contract in `observation.*`; `observation.inSessionDelivery=true` only when the current Codex orchestrator session was launched through `task-board codex`\n- `observation.runtime` / `observation.delivery` report the orchestrator runtime and its non-blocking completion channel: `codex-session`/`codex-in-session` (app-server push), `claude-session`/`claude-background-wait` (harness background wait wakes the session), or `external`/`external-watch`\n- Spawn run events use replay cursor tokens (`RUN-ID:seq`) so local CLI, TUI, and remote watchers can resume from the same durable position\n- Spawn directives are first-class durable records with acknowledgement state; use `spawn directive` / `spawn directives` instead of ad hoc notes for cancel/pause/restart/reroute intent\n- Goal-bound directive output always includes the full current `active_goal`; each new directive retains its immutable issued `goal_ref`, and legacy records report unknown context rather than guessing\n- Provider usage/budget limits, transient launch/transport failures, nonzero goal-owner exits, unmet role-handoff status/evidence, missing reviewer verdict branches, and unmet delivery acceptance create a same-revision autonomous successor instead of a terminal failed owner\n- If successor log preparation or runner startup fails, the successor stays non-terminal `queued` with a persisted deferred-start retry lease. Status/wait/follow observation reacquires an expired lease and retries the same successor without duplicating ownership; never treat a failed start attempt as loss of the same-revision owner.\n- A role-handoff goal completes only at its immutable role end status with a new or updated task-scoped outcome artifact. Zero exit while parked in `analysis`/`development` or without that evidence is recoverable and queues the same revision again.\n- Required/light delivery acceptance is `done` plus a producer outcome and a later reviewer run with a persisted evidence-backed `accepted` branch. `changes_requested`, Stop-The-Line, and branchless reviewer exits do not count as acceptance. `review=none` still requires `done`, producer outcome evidence, and an orchestrator evidence check after the latest producer run\n- Provider `blocked` and board `blocked` role exits share one evidence gate. Only board `blocked` plus a new or updated task-scoped Stop-The-Line packet may terminate with `completion_reason=stop_boundary`; this is never successful delivery. Missing evidence queues the same revision for recovery.\n- Watcher notifications are external observation surfaces by default; `task-board codex` adds runtime-native terminal delivery back into the active Codex thread\n- Claude orchestrator sessions (`CLAUDECODE` env set) have no injection API; the non-blocking channel is the harness itself — run each printed `task-board spawn wait RUN-...` as a harness background task (`run_in_background`), fan out one background wait per run, and react as each completion wakes the session. See [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) Pattern E\n- Single-child variant: fused `task-board spawn TASK ... --wait` registers the tracked run identically and blocks in-process to terminal state with `spawn wait` exit semantics — one background Bash call per child; the `run_in_background` wrapper itself remains a caller/harness decision. See [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) \"Fused Spawn And Wait\"\n- Soft directives are cooperative checkpoint signals only. Spawned agents can inspect them through `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` + `task-board spawn directives \"$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID\"`; do not assume live in-session push\n- Coordinators must not wrap a plain `task-board spawn` in `&`, `nohup`, or runtime background wrappers; `--background` already returns a tracked run. The exception is the fused blocking `spawn ... --wait` barrier, which a Claude orchestrator deliberately runs as a harness background task\n- Spawn validates that the board task brief is usable: description and acceptance criteria must be real, not placeholders or one-line fluff\n- Repeated spawn on the same task must be idempotent: existing injected checklist items are not duplicated\n- When auditing spawn docs/help/source, exclude `.temp/**` from repo-wide searches so stale task-scoped worktrees do not look like current contract files\n\n### Review Policy\n\nEvery board element has a `review` policy. Set it at creation with `create(..., review=required|light|none)`, change it with `set_details(ID, review=...)`, and inspect it through the `review` q projection. New elements and existing elements without the field default to `required`.\n\n- `required` keeps the current producer → reviewer → rework/reviewer loop. A producer handoff at `to-review` must be routed to a reviewer child, and only an accepted reviewer verdict reaches `done`.\n- `light` still requires a reviewer child, but reviewer selection should favor a cheaper effort tier that can satisfy the same evidence and quality gates. This is orchestration guidance, not a hard CLI enforcement rule.\n- `none` lets the Orchestrator accept a producer handoff without spawning a reviewer child. After checking the task-specific acceptance criteria, checklist, validation results, and outcome evidence, the Orchestrator may move the element from `to-review` directly to `done` with `set_status`. Missing or inadequate evidence must be routed back to the producer; `none` never means accepting an unevidenced handoff.\n\nThis subsection qualifies unconditional reviewer-routing guidance elsewhere in the workflow: that guidance describes the default `required` policy. Human review is not introduced by any policy value.\n\n### Model Selection Guide\n\n**Agent selection happens before model selection.** Inspect `project_config()` first. Canonical `preferred_agentic_system.exclusive` pins one provider; `mixed` permits an explicit in-set `--agent` or the current in-set affinity. Out-of-set requests fail before side effects. When no canonical system exists, legacy usable `preferred_agent` and then runtime affinity preserve compatibility. Do not hardcode a provider in reusable orchestration instructions.\n\nProject ceilings constrain the already selected provider; they never replace it. Canonical provider-system membership does not require a ceiling, while legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` still requires the corresponding provider ceiling to be usable. After provider selection, the matching `spawn.ceilings.<agent>` policy and optional role override pin or constrain model and, for Codex or Qwen, reasoning effort.\n\n**`model_criterion=equal` exception.** Inspect the effective target agent/role ceiling in `project_config()` first. When it reports `model_criterion=equal`, skip the per-spawn model selection assessment entirely: do not add `Spawn selection assessment` paragraphs to task notes or resources, and do not pass `--selection-rationale` or `--ack-resolved`. Use the exact configured model and the configured Codex or Qwen reasoning effort, when present, as the pinned pair, passing every required model/effort flag explicitly. A single short note naming the pinned pair at the first spawn is allowed; do not repeat it for later spawns.\n\n**Ordered-criterion deliberate selection.** For `less_or_equal` or `greater_or_equal`, write exactly one non-empty rationale line and pass it as `--selection-rationale`. If the request passes through, spawn directly and omit `--ack-resolved`. If policy changes model or effort, the CLI records `(requested_pair, rationale)` in task notes and returns the typed requested-to-resolved checkpoint without creating a run. Deliberately reassess the resolved pair. If it remains fit, re-invoke with exact `--ack-resolved <model>/<effort>` and a fresh rationale line for the resolved pair; after whitespace normalization that text must differ byte-for-byte from every rationale previously recorded for the same task and role. The accepted tuple is `(resolved_pair, fresh_rationale)` and the manifest carries the tuple history. If the resolved pair is inadequate, request another in-bounds pair or escalate the ceiling constraint. Content quality remains the caller's responsibility; the CLI enforces structural pair binding and freshness rather than semantic text analysis.\n\n```bash\n# First ordered request; rationale is recorded when the pair passes through.\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max --selection-rationale \"gpt-5.6-terra at max fits the bounded implementation and validation burden\"\n\n# After a reported adjustment to gpt-5.6-luna/max, reassess and acknowledge that exact pair.\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max --selection-rationale \"gpt-5.6-luna at max remains adequate for this bounded change and its test gates\" --ack-resolved gpt-5.6-luna/max\n```\n\n**Choose explicitly for maximum task quality per token.** Before every board spawn, assess scope, complexity, risk, required autonomy, validation burden, and expected context and token cost. Then select the strongest suitable pair that can pass the existing quality gates; do not reflexively spend the strongest model or highest supported effort.\n\n| Task type | Agent | Model | --reasoning-effort | Notes |\n|-----------|-------|-------|--------------------|-------|\n| Cross-module, novel, risky, or review-critical work | codex | `gpt-5.6-sol` | **max** | Highest recommended board-managed effort; preserve predictable delegation in task-board |\n| Balanced agentic work | codex | `gpt-5.6-terra` | **max** | Strong fit for routine work with meaningful autonomy or validation |\n| Fast, affordable agentic work | codex | `gpt-5.6-luna` | **max** | Luna does not support `ultra` |\n| Complex coding and research | codex | `gpt-5.5` | **xhigh** | Does not support `max` or `ultra` |\n| Everyday coding | codex | `gpt-5.4` | **xhigh** | Strong general coding option for narrower scope |\n| Simpler cost-sensitive work | codex | `gpt-5.4-mini` | **xhigh** | Use only when bounded scope and clear validation retain the same quality gate |\n| Ultra-fast code iteration | codex | `gpt-5.3-codex-spark` | **xhigh** | Speed-oriented coding model |\n| Hardest, longest-running Claude work | claude | `claude-fable-5` | - | Most capable Claude model; `--model` is still required; Claude rejects reasoning effort |\n| Everyday complex Claude work | claude | `claude-opus-4-8` | - | Recommended Claude default for board children |\n| Routine Claude work | claude | `claude-sonnet-5` | - | Efficient for routine, well-scoped tasks |\n| Fast, cheap Claude checks | claude | `claude-haiku-4-5` | - | Fastest for quick answers and bounded checks |\n| Balanced Qwen Token Plan work | qwen | `qwen3.7-plus` | **xhigh** | Recommended registered Qwen Code model; query current context and Team seat/quota metadata |\n| Highest-capability Qwen preview work | qwen | `qwen3.8-max-preview` | **xhigh** | Preview model; use only when the preview tradeoff fits the task |\n\nFor board-managed work, `max` is the highest recommended effort. `ultra` remains explicitly compatible on supported models, but it enables model-internal delegation rather than deeper reasoning. Prefer task-board's tracked `RUN-...` lifecycle, role assignment, dependencies, and reviewer routing as the predictable delegation plane. Lower-cost pairs are good choices only when they can still pass the same required tests, build/validation, outcome, and review gates.\n\nCurrent Codex values are `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh` (UI: Extra high), `max`, and `ultra`. Compatibility is model-specific; query `models(agent=codex)` before launch. Deprecated model IDs may retain legacy `minimal`, but it is not a current recommendation.\n\nCurrent Qwen Code client values are `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`, and `max`; task-board passes the selected tier through Qwen's stream-JSON initialize control frame. Query `models(agent=qwen)` for registered model IDs, Qwen Code context windows, supported effort values, and current Token Plan Team seat pricing, monthly quotas, frequency-limit semantics, and plan applicability. Qwen ordinary child runs are tracked like Codex/Claude runs, but Qwen goal-bound owner launches are not supported.\n\n**Budget: unlimited by default.** Don't pass `--budget` unless the user explicitly asks to cap spending. Let agents finish their work — a $5 cap on a $3 task means a failed half-done agent + a retry that costs $6 total. Unlimited is cheaper than retries.\n\nThe CLI blocks missing model, missing Codex/Qwen reasoning, reasoning on Claude, and known incompatible model/effort pairs before board lookup or mutation. Unknown explicit model IDs remain forward-compatible and produce a warning; known wrong-agent model IDs also warn.\n\n### Working on a Task (as sub-agent)\n\n1. Estimate + assign + start → `task-board m 'set_estimate(TASK-260218-abc, estimate=estimated, scale=fibonacci, number=5); assign(TASK-260218-abc, agent=agent-name); set_status(TASK-260218-abc, status=development)'`\n2. Work until the task is genuinely complete or objectively blocked. Do not stop with the task parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing` just because one local action finished.\n3. Stop and escalate if completion would require a forced fit: document the constraint, failed assumption, viable options, and exact human/product/architecture decision needed. Do not pile on compensating tests, stubs, flags, or priority rules to make an invalid model appear to work.\n4. Update checklist → `task-board m 'check_item(TASK-260218-abc, item=1)'`\n5. Save results → `task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=TASK-260218-abc_results.md, content=\"...\", type=outcome, description=\"Implementation notes\")'`\n6. Ready for review → `task-board m 'set_status(TASK-260218-abc, status=to-review)'`\n7. Or blocked → only for a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only stop-the-line decision; attach the evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact decision or external input needed, then set `blocked`\n8. Orchestrator confirms producer evidence, spawns a tracked reviewer child (`task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`), waits or monitors the returned `RUN-...`, then routes by the reviewer-child verdict: accepted → `done`; changes requested → the appropriate producer through `to-dev` or `analysis`, followed by a new reviewer; genuine stop-the-line boundary → `blocked` with the full evidence packet\n\nHuman-facing language is part of the contract: a producer ending at `to-review` says \"ready for review\" or \"handed off to review\", not \"done\"/\"complete\"/\"finished\"/\"final\"/\"готово\". Only a reviewer/coordinator may call the board task done, and only after the board status is actually `done`.\n\n### Dependencies\n\nTask/bug dependencies are execution ground truth. An unfinished stored\n`blockedBy` link makes `isBlocked=true`, and status enforcement refuses to start\nthat task or bug. For story/epic containers, only an unfinished explicitly\nstored manual link is a hard block and makes `isBlocked=true`.\n\nNew Task/Bug records also require an explicit estimated value before their\nfirst transition into `development`. Query `{ estimate }` to inspect the nested\nenum and use `set_estimate(...)` to change it. An absent Estimate section is a\nlegacy persistence state and does not activate the gate retroactively.\nStory and Epic estimates are never persisted or mutated. Their `{ estimate }`\nprojection rolls up descendant Task/Bug values to `estimated(fibonacci(sum))`,\nor to `partiallyEstimated` with the known `rolledEstimate`, an `N/total`\n`notEstimatedCount`, and sorted `unestimatedTaskIds`.\n\nCross-container waiting derived from the live task DAG is advisory scheduling\nstate: it is never persisted, never changes `isBlocked`, and never adds a\ncontainer-level status-enforcement rule. Query projections expose active\nadvisory waiting as `isAdvisoryBlocked=true` and identify its source through\n`derivedBlockedBy`. `summary()` and `list(blocked=true)` report both hard and\nadvisory entries and annotate each with `isBlocked` and\n`isAdvisoryBlocked`; a mixed container has both flags set. Use these annotations\nbefore deciding whether an orchestrator is seeing manual intent or computed\nordering.\n\n```bash\n# TASK-B depends on TASK-A\ntask-board m 'link(TASK-260218-def, blocked_by=TASK-260218-abc)'\n\n# CLI will refuse to move TASK-B to development while TASK-A is not done\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260218-def, status=development)'\n# Error: cannot start TASK-260218-def — blocked by: TASK-260218-abc\n\n# Remove dependency\ntask-board m 'unlink(TASK-260218-def, blocked_by=TASK-260218-abc)'\n\n# Inspect hard and advisory scheduling constraints together\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(blocked=true) { id isBlocked isAdvisoryBlocked blockedBy derivedBlockedBy }'\n```\n\n### Board Review\n\n```bash\n# Queries (use --format compact for token efficiency)\ntask-board q 'schema()'                                                          # discover fields, presets, operations\ntask-board q 'project_config()'                                                  # confirm runtime, launch gate, and model criterion before agent work\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary()'                                        # quick overview\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260218-abc) { full }'                    # full element details\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { overview }'  # what's in progress\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, blocked=true) { id name status }'  # what's blocked\ntask-board q --format compact 'agents()'                                         # agent dashboard\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan(EPIC-260218-abc, mode=children)'             # execution waves (super-compact planning shape)\ntask-board q 'plan(scope=[EPIC-260218-abc, TASK-260218-def], mode=children)'     # plan over a set of subtrees (union)\n\n# Full-text search (local only)\ntask-board grep \"AudioRecorder\"\n\n# Validation (local only)\ntask-board validate\ntask-board repair-links\n```\n\n---\n\n## Querying & Mutating the Board\n\nThree primary access modes following the [agent-facing-api](https://github.com/relux-works/skill-agent-facing-api) pattern — all through one CLI binary:\n\n- **`q` (query DSL)** — structured reads: `task-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { overview }'`\n- **`grep` (full-text search)** — scoped text search: `task-board grep \"AudioRecorder\"` (local only)\n- **`m` (mutation DSL)** — structured writes: `task-board m 'set_status(TASK-260218-abc, status=done)'`\n\nPlus a CLI plan facade for pretty-print / render / save: `task-board plan ...`, and local-only utilities such as `validate` (structure check).\n\nField presets: `minimal` (id, status), `default` (id, name, status), `overview` (+ assignee, parent), `full` (all 27 fields in the current schema; use `schema()` for the evolving list), `progress` (workflow/progress fields, including estimate), `planning` (status, dependencies, parent).\n\nWhen mentioning board elements in human-facing responses, always include both the concrete ID and the human-readable title/name. This applies to epics, stories, tasks, and bugs. ID-only references are not enough for people scanning the chat because they should not have to resolve identifiers manually.\n\nFor DSL operations, mutations reference, field projection, and decision table — see [`references/querying.md`](references/querying.md).\n\n---\n\n## Tips\n\n- **Detail stories before planning** — create tasks for all stories before phase planning, so the plan shows real scope\n- **AC must be verifiable** — \"works\" is bad, \"test passes\" is good\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
